Food to blame for European inflation
The main reason for the rise in inflation measured in the eurozone is the rapidly rising food prices.
In the 13 countries of the eurozone, inflation was higher than expected in November - after the 2.6 per cent in October, an annual inflation rate of 3.1 percent was registered, as reported by the Statistical Office of the EU in Luxembourg.
Official projections are down from just 3 percent, Bloomberg reported.
In contrast to the American, British, and Canadian central banks, the European Central Bank (ECB) has not lowered the key interest rate. According to its discretion, the rate cut would favour rising commodity prices and the slight drop in unemployment the emergence of an inflationary spiral.
The ECB is eager to keep the inflation rate below two percent, or at least narrowly at the two percent mark.
According to an analyst, the ECB is in a quandary: one would have to "contrude" inflation in order to rule out the need for action later.
At 3.3 percent, the inflation rated a twelve-year record high in Germany in November; in Spain, the rate climbed from 3.6 to 4.1 percent.
Increased food and energy prices are responsible for the rise in eurozone inflation.
MATIF wheat quotations rose 88 per cent, and soybean price also hit a record high since 1973.
There was also a significant increase in inflation in edible oil and dairy products in 2007.
The world market price for crude oil rose 52 percent this year - last month, the price per barrel of black gold reached nearly $100.
According to the ECB, inflation will rise from 2.1 percent to 2.5 percent in 2008, but 2009 is expected to fall to 1.9 percent.
For the coming 3-4 months, analysts expect a 3 percent inflation rate, but estimate the annual average rate at 2.1 percent.
For 2008, experts expect the ECB to cut interest rates twice.
Gallup locates government crisis
After long stagnation, the number of Fidesz sympathizers has grown significantly by the beginning of December. For the first time since 2002, the party has had the largest base camp, while approval for the Socialists has reached an unprecedented low of 13 percent, according to Gallup.
In the case of the party loyal voters, the difference is clear thanks to the high determination of the opposition supporters: More than two-thirds (71 percent) face one-fifth (20 percent) approval for the MSZP.
MDF and SZDSZ are under the parliamentary entry hurdle, according to the survey: They come to two percent in the total population.
The number of party voters has grown by one percent in both camps.
Compared to the last low point, overall economic sentiment has deteriorated further - never before has the proportion of those who judge the country's economic situation as "extremely bad," so high (41 percent), while another 46 per cent consider it "quite bad."
After more hopefuled prospects in the spring and summer, the families see the change in their financial situation again more pessimistic.
For the coming year, the majority of the population expects a further deterioration of the situation; only one-tenth assumes an improvement.
A Gallup poll conducted in December found that 71% of respondents - a historic high - judged the prime minister's work to be "rather bad than right."
Thirteen percent of the respondents have great confidence or confidence in Ferenc Gyurcsány in general, while the proportion of advocates for Viktor Orbán represents 38 percent.
Around 54% don't trust the head of government at all, compared with 27% distrust against Fidesz's party leader.
One of the indicators of political sentiment is the number of those who think you can get to grips with dissatisfaction with the prime minister even without a change of government.
After the riots last October, 35% had advocated the whereabouts of the government and the head of government, today this share amounts to only 20%, while the relative majority (33 percent) sees the solution only in new elections.
Asked about their interest in the People's Vote, 60 percent replied that they were "certainly" participating, and another 20% would "probably" cast their vote.
By comparison, if the parliamentary elections were held this Sunday, only 55 percent would "certainly" go to the polls, another 15% consider their participation "probable." Given these tendencies, the outcome of the popular vote would be foreseeable.
74% would vote against the tuition fees, 79% against the practice fee, and 84% would vote against the hospital daily allowance.
Applied to the group of participants in the referendum "certainly", the proportions of opponents of the tuition, practice and hospital fees would even be 81, 84 or 89 percent.
Also most expensive, Tesco increased by almost a fifth
Hypermarkets in the Budaor trade area also maintained the average price increase of 16% from last year this year, with the difference that there was no increase in VAT this year.
Articles from Napi Gazdaság
In the Budaörs trade triangle, the price of the usual Christmas basket of the "Napi" has also increased by 16 percent this year, but price increases of over 1,000 HUF have been registered in all hypermarkets.
After prices in food markets "galloped away" last December, inflation has not been drastic in percentage terms, compared to the already high, earlier baseline level.
However, it certainly deterrents to consumers that what they paid well below 9,000 HUF last year, isn’t even available for 10,000 HUF this year.
The price of the "Napi" shopping cart exceeded the magic 10,000 HUF limit by at least 91 HUF in the markets surveyed.
The highest price was $10,573.
(In 6800 the price was 6,800 HUF.)
The prices for unicum and live fish remained almost at the previous year, while the prices of turkey breast fillets, nut and poppies have risen sharply.
Prices for flour and bread corresponded to the dates of the Central Statistical Office of Hungary, but at Auchan there were 1kg bread wakes for 139 forints.
There was no competitive price plunge to below 200 forints on the product.
Also surprising here was the price of a two-liter Coca-Cola deposit bottle of less than 200 forints, so the drink including deposit cost only 257 HUF.
Nevertheless, this year in the "Napi" shopping cart comparison Auchan scored the most expensively, and Cora scored the cheapest.
Our calculations have shown that Cora increased prices in the "Napi" shopping cart by 15 compared to Christmas prices last year, while Tesco and Auchan each increased by 18 percent.
The difference between last year's cheapest shopping cart and today's most expensive was 12%, while the increase between last year's cheapest shopping cart and the most expensive of the current year accounted for 21%.
The average price increase was 1,480.00 HUF.
The average price increase in the previous year was 1,206.00 forints, but the VAT increase was noticeable at the prices at last Christmas.
Exactly this process has affected the trade badly: apparently the department stores could not really decide which price range they should apply to the individual articles, since the impact of the price increase on consumer behavior was unforeseeable.
(If the retailer poorly plans the speed of circulation of a product, it can fall by substantial gains in the price range, which depends on the traffic intensity.)
Due to VAT, the inflation of the "Napi" shopping cart last year was so radical that the most expensive basket exceeded the value from 2006 around 1720 Forint, whereas the price difference in the cheapest basket was only 844 forints.
This year, such extremes weren't observed, as the most expensive basket cost around 1,630.00 forint more than in the previous year, and even with the cheapest, there was a price increase of 1,284.00 forints.
Comparing the last two Christmas seasons, a catastrophic price increase showed, the price of daily consumables in the hypermarkets has risen by more than 30 percent in less than 20 months.
(This, in turn, does not necessarily confirm the data from the Central Statistical Office of Hungary: for some staples, the Statistical Office found more than 30 percent inflation over a period of 12 months.
In addition to flour also with eggs.)
However, if you take a look at the "Napi" shopping cart, this seems almost impossible with the latter, since the price level of the product, which was already expensive in the previous year, has risen by barely more than 10 percent even in the worst case.
Obviously, flour suppliers find themselves in a stronger position than the egg suppliers - after all, they were able to pass the drastic price increase onto the multinationals.
In the golden trade triangle, the hypermarkets do not complain; it is constantly reported by good years.
Our experience shows that the majority of customers in the three department stores are no longer looking at prices.
Large quantities are purchased from all large quantities, durable food goes in pallets over the counters.
Wednesday morning in the morning, there were endless queues in all three department stores at the ticket office when suddenly the number of customers exploded.
However, a lower customer frequency is reported by the country, so that many multinationals complain to pollsters that they could not even reach the planned sales, not only for food, but also for sustainable consumer goods.
In many places, disinfectants are saved
This year, more than a thousand infections have been registered in hospitals due to improper handling of antibiotics.
The hygienic conditions in the hospitals, the overcrowding of the beds, the overloading of the staff - all factors that are not exactly conducive to the containment of the infections, according to the senior doctor of the National Epidemiology Center.
In many places, disinfectants are also saved.
An MRSA infection has recently been diagnosed in the Traumatology Department of János Hospital.
The bacterium resistant to antibiotics was found in a male patient who was hospitalized after a serious accident.
The patient was isolated.
At the beginning of the year there was already a similar infection here.
The injured of a serious car accident was discharged from the hospital, but a little later the man had to return with an MRSA infection. An important operation could not be carried out since then because of the bacterium, which is still located in the patient's body.
Until December 10 of this year, 1094 infections caused by multidrug-resistant pathogens were reported to the National Nosocomialis Surveillance System from hospitals, according to the senior physician of the National Epidemiology Center, Karolina Böröcz.
So many infections caused by multi-resistant pathogens were certainly registered in the hospitals, but the obscurity figure is likely to be higher.
About two-thirds of infections (683 cases) were triggered by MRSA, Staphylococcus aureus, which is resistant to methicillin-oxacillin.
The Staphylococcus aureus (SA) bacterium carries about one-third of people in their nose without causing a disease.
SA-related infections (such as sore throat, tonsillitis, inflammation of the skin or wounds) usually react well to antibiotics, they are easily curable.
However, if the agents are too highly dosed or used improperly, antibiotic-resistants are produced from the well-responding bacteria, which spread rapidly under appropriate conditions in hospitals.
Especially in patients with weakened or permanently poorly functioning immune systems, but also in elderly, or patients with low body weight, these bacteria can cause serious infections, such as wound infection or blood poisoning, i.e. sepsis.
According to the medical director of the János hospital, the MRSA infection is nothing special, such incidents are part of everyday hospital life.
One should not confuse the infection with the epidemic, warned Tibor Nyulasi - MRSA does not exist only where it is not sought.
Resistant to antibiotics
"Such patients are isolated, the nursing staff wears rubber gloves and masks in care, and we have the antibiotics necessary for treatment," according to Tibor Nyulasi.
When being admitted to the hospital, patients are examined for MRSA, an infection cannot always be avoided.
Sampling too rare
The majority of infections caused by MRSA in hospitals would be preventable, but the currently prevailing conditions and circumstances are unfavourable for curbing the emergence or the spread of the multi-resistant pathogen, says Karolina Böröcz.
Since this infection occurs predominantly in hospitals and retirement homes, the hospitals should examine the admitted patients and immediately isolate MRSA-infected patients.
However, microbiological sampling is not practiced as frequently in Hungarian hospitals.
Not least because there are no grants for this investigation on the part of the state health insurance company.
Most cases would therefore only come to light when the patient already has a noticeable MRSA infection, according to the medical director of the epidemiology center.
There are strict recommendations from the European Union on the appropriate use of antibiotics in human medicine, but there are also directives in Hungary for the treatment of certain infections and diseases.
Antibiotic therapies should be targeted, i.e. microbiological examinations are necessary so that no resistance to the agent can form, but this directive is not always adhered to by hospitals.
Among the outpatients or patients in primary care, microbiological sampling is even rarer, according to Karolina Böröcz.
Saved on disinfectants
Scientific evidence demonstrates that MRSA is most commonly transmitted by nursing staff from one patient to another.
This is precisely why hand washing or wearing gloves and masks are of great importance, but there are also considerable deficiencies in hospitals in this area.
MRSA is transmitted by droplet infection or contact, but there is not always the possibility of isolating the infected patient.
The rooms are even often crowded, the beds narrowly lined up, although there should be a distance of at least 1.5 meters between them from an epidemiological point of view.
As Karolina Böröcz further notes, it unfortunately even happens in the financially less well-placed hospitals that disinfectants are saved, there are few nurses, many patients - all factors that almost favor the emergence of infections.
Such infections are demonstrabled to be far less frequent there, or the further spread of the pathogen can be stopped, where these protective measures are adhered to, according to Karolina Böröcz.
Government programme against MRSA
Gettinginging MRSA infections causes enormous costs for health insurance companies, so they are combated everywhere.
In England, for example, the prevention of MRSA or its containment has been recorded in the government programme.
According to initial indications, the programme is successful - as published data shows, the tendency of MRSA infections is declining.
The European Centre for Disease Prevention (ECDC) has declared the prevention of the emergence of multidrug-resistant pathogens (including MRSA) and the prevention of infections in hospitals one of its priorities.
Death handlebars police chief wasn't drunk
In the case of the car accident with a fatal outcome caused by the police chief of the XXII district, the official report is now available.
The now-resigned XXII district police chief, who killed a pedestrian in a car accident on Tuesday, was neither alcoholized nor under the influence of drugs, said Attila Morvai, the press spokesman for Budapest's chief prosecutor's office on Friday after the official opinion on the case was presented.
The opinion confirmed the results of the initial investigation, according to which the police chief had not consumed alcohol, before he sat at the wheel.
The former police chief boarded with his own car in the XXIIth district into Kossuth Lajos street and in the dresupposite, crossing a 55-year-old man who crossed the road at an undisclosed location.
(According to StVO, however, this is not a mitigating circumstance - the handlebars, even if pedestrians do not cross the road at the zebra crossing, subordence.)
The pedestrian succumbed to his injuries after being admitted to the hospital.
According to police reports, the driver suffered a shock, then resigned his functions and asked for retirement.
The Budapest public prosecutor's office has opened its investigation into the incident.
In addition, Attila Morvai mentioned that the former police chief had cooperated with the investigators from the beginning.
Another EUR 400 million for infrastructure
Finance Minister János Veres and Marta Gajecka, vice-president of the European Investment Bank (EIB), signed three loan agreements worth a total of €400 million in Budapest on Friday. The money is to flow into education, transport and environmental infrastructure and the energy industry.
In 2006, the European Investment Bank (EIB) approved a €300 million framework for the financing of transport and environmental projects implemented with grants from the Cohesion Fund. The €100 €100 € contract has already been signed earlier, the current agreement is about the use of €200 million.
The sum of €150 million agreed in the loan agreement is also to be spent on the budgetary financing of educational projects.
The EIB has approved a total of €300 million for this purpose for Hungary and the use of the remaining €150 million will be decided later as part of the extension of the credit agreement.
In the field of innovation, the contracting parties have signed an agreement on EUR 50 million, which will flow mainly into research and development programmes at the University of Debrecen.
At the meeting, the metro line 4 was also discussed.
It was agreed that once Hungary has clarified the details of metro funding with the EU, the original treaty and eventual modifications will be re-discussed.
According to János Veres, this would be possible in the first quarter of 2008.
Since 1990, the EIB has financed a total €8.4 billion of projects in Hungary.
The loan rates are EURIBOR-linked, the loan is provided commission-free by the EIB with an 8-year deferral.
Bulgaria, the real estate paradise of the EU
The development of the Bulgarian real estate market is fabulous - the whole of Europe looks attentively to the formerly rarely mentioned Balkan state.
What, where, and who buys in the still cheapest market of the EU, but which is rapidly becoming more expensive?
An analysis by the Bulgarian Re/Max Division.
Bulgaria is undoubtedly the most sought-after pavement for property purchases in Eastern Europe, with cheap ski apartments and houses by the sea.
Between the mountains, the regions of Bansko, Borovec, Pamprovo and Vitosa are among the most popular, on the sea are Albena, the "Gold Coast", the "Sun Coast", and the historic towns of Sozopol and Nessebar, but properties around Kavarna and Balcik are increasingly in demand.
According to assessments by Re/Max analysts in Bulgaria, the interior will sooner or later attract investors, for example Veliko Tarnovo, Gabrovo, Elena, and Triavna, and the extremely favorable area of Russe also records more buyers.
The capital Sofia also offers tempting offers - commercial and office space offers the highest returns.
Bulgaria is on track to become one of Europe's most attractive golfing paradises, and thus attracting tourists in addition to the summer and winter seasons in the other two seasons.
Forward-looking investors are therefore already buying on a large scale, for example around Bansko.
According to analysts, people who acquire real estate in Bulgaria are also striving to change their way of life.
Most buyers are English, but there are also interested parties from France, Belgium, Spain, and more and more from Romania.
Investors from Malta and Cyprus are also present, while there are predominantly Russian buyers in the Black Sea and in the ski resorts.
There are three groups of foreign buyers, says Georgij Jankov, regional director of Re/Max Bulgaria.
Those who want to settle either buy revitalized real estate around 50-100,000 euros, or run-down houses for 5-15,000 euros, which they then renovate themselves.
Good connection and accessibility as well as communication possibilities are very important, but the proximity to a big city, shops and doctors are also crucial for the buyers.
The second category includes those who are looking for a holiday home (Bulgaria was listed amon the top ten holiday destinations of Germans in December).
The third group make up business people who are investing with the aim of medium to long-term value appreciation.
Properties in the capital, however, are constantly becoming more expensive due to the ever-increasing demand.
Within the EU, property prices are the lowest in Bulgaria, but in the past five years there has been an annual price increase of 21 percent.
The increase in real estate prices far exceeds the increase in salaries.
Fodor and the six Hungarian percentages
As Environment Minister Gábor Fodor announced, the expert meetings come to an end on Tuesday, and the debate on the international climate regime after 2012 will continue by ministers on Wednesday after the official opening.
Experts and politicians from 192 countries are discussinging Bali's roadmap, which lays down the emission-reducing measures for the post-2012 period, i.e. after the Kyoto Protocol has expired.
A fierce debate between developed and developing countries is currently underway.
The U.S. has already signalled that it doesn't want to negotiate concrete figures, but it would be willing to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 25-40 percent by 2020 compared to 1990.
The other big question of the conference is whether developed countries are prepared to support developing countries both financially and technologically in addition to reducing emissions.
An agreement has also been reached to support developing countries.
The Adjustment Fund, which is subject to the Kyoto Protocol, is to be managed by a 16-member Council, which is expected to be found at the UN's Global Environment Facility (GEF).
Hungary's six percent
Environment Minister Gábor Fodor, on behalf of Hungary, supports the EU's aspiration to maintain its pioneering role in global climate action and to reach a new international agreement by 2009.
The minister stated that the EU will reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 20% by 2020 (as measured by 1990 levels), regardless of whether or not the "Accord" was successfully reached. The Union would even be prepared to respond to a 30% reduction if other large industrialised countries in the world or developing countries are also prepared to meet their commitments to the best of their ability and responsibly.
Gábor Fodor emphasized that the objective of the states that joined the Kyoto Protocol in 1997 was a reduction of only 5.2 percent, whereas the EU with its then 15 member states had already committed to a minus of 8%.
The requirement for Hungary is reduction of greenhouse gas emissions by 6% compared to the years 1985-87 - a requirement that the country will meet, as can be read in the press release of the Ministry of the Environment.
The deployment is high in Bali - after all, the long-term international climate policy agreement must be completed within two years, otherwise it would certainly only come into force after 2012. In this case, the reduction projects laid down in the Kyoto Protocol would not be followed by a new, international emission reduction system, the importance of which was also highlighted by the IPCC, the UN's international Nobel Peace Prize-winning scientific climate change committee in its most recent report.
As a result, emission reduction would be delayed, and increasing the Earth’s average temperature by more than 2°C would be unavoidable, resulting in extremely serious and irreparable consequences.
British army advertises on YouTube
Unitries of the British army have published recordings of operations in Iraq and Afghanistan to increase popularity on the video portal.
First of all, the British Air Force (RAF) has discovered YouTube for itself: Since March 2006, special forces stationed in Afghanistan have been uploading videos about Taliban-led operations to the net, reports the BBC.
The recordings with miniature cameras attached to helmets are inspected at the air base in Kandahar and then sent to London, where they are placed on the Internet.
Political message unavoidable
Army soldiers have already contributed 139 such shots, their "channel" has been viewed more than 75,000 times.
Britain's Ministry of Defence Headquarters was registered under "defenceheadquarters" in the spring of 2007 to publish videos about Iraqi and Afghan operations, but the British Foreign Office (Commonwealth Office) and international peacekeepers (MNFI) stationed in Iraq also have their own YouTube channels.
Deployment in Afghanistan
Originally, the Air Air Force has published the videos with the aim of recruiting soldiers, and as the Ministry of Defense says, the contributions also serve the purpose of informing the population about the actual targets of foreign operations.
According to defense expert Gordon Mackenzie to the BBC, propaganda published in this kind is almost free of charge compared to conventional television advertisements, but these videos always carry a certain amount of political message, as they confirm the right to exist of the military measures adopted by the government in their own way.
Americans advertise with game
However, the army must also fight against amateur recordings, which in addition to the carefully edited, censorated recordings also make it into the net - such as those that show the death of the soldiers: In the somewhat hypocritical, "official" propaganda films, the losses are discussed at most, but the bloody sides of the war are not shown.
Incidentally, from the theatres of war in Iraq and Afghanistan, numerous videos that have already been filmed in use have already been uploaded to the platform, but this "genus" has only recently been discovered for themselves.
The Americans, however, advertise in a slightly different way, namely with the free downloadable shooting game "America's Army", in which the player can slip into the skin of the soldier as part of a more or less life-living simulation.
Christmas party with tombola and pogatsche in the ministries
Labour Minister Mónika Lamperth is by no means squeamish: instead of a Christmas party, she organizes a working conference at which her employees can end the year 2007 with coffee and pogatsche.
Some ministries celebrate with live music, others conjure Christmas spirit with tombola or toasts into the civil servants' hearts.
Party, Christmas spirits, concerts and buffet - that's how power celebrates.
Christmas fever has already gripped the offices.
The largest Christmas party of a ministry will be held this year in the defense department, but the employees of the ministry are not expected.
At the "Christmas party of the orphans", children are gifted by fallen soldiers, at the "mission celebration" family members are hosted by soldiers currently stationed abroad, but there is also a reception for the press.
The department provides around 25 million for the festivities.
Compared to other departments, the National Agency for Development (NFÜ) and the Ministry of Economic Affairs and Transport are beating over the strands.
The 4.32-million-dollar Christmas party of the Ministry of Economic Affairs will be helded at the house of the future, about 350 of the 600 employees are expected.
The focus of the festivities is on Christmas, but there will also be live acts and a tombola raffle.
The National Agency for Development (NFÜ) held its 4.7-million-dollar Christmas party on Wednesday at the House of the State Justice Council (OIT), with concerts and a feast.
The hall rent amounted to 1 million forints, the rest was spent on the more than 400 invited guests.
According to our information, a department of the NFÜ is also invited to another Christmas party organized by the Hungarian Economic Development Center (MAG Zrt) from EU funds.
For the celebration, the building of the Lustspieltheater ("Vígszínház") was rented.
MAG Zrt has not confirmed this information as we have not reached those responsible.
What the suit for?
The OIT building was not only open to the guests of the NFÜ, but also to the Ministry of Judiciary and Policing, which held the Lucia Ball there instead of a Christmas party on December 7.
They did not have to pay rent to the OIT for using the premises.
Around 500 visitors came to the ball.
The festivities were opened with an address by the minister, after the dinner there was live music for dancing.
At the request of [origo] it was announced that there was no ball gown or suit obligation on the ball.
The ministries of health, work, and self-government are each preparing for puritanical celebration.
The Ministry of Health organizes the celebration for the employees within their own four walls for around 400,000 forints.
Labour Minister Mónika Lamperth doesn’t officially invite her staff to a Christmas party, but to the conference at the end of the year.
One expects about 180 guests, who - according to the press department - are only served with coffee and pogatsche, so the celebration goes out with 350,000 forints.
Self-Governing Minister Gordon Bajnai and his colleagues will simply toast in the auditorium of the ministry.
The Ministry of Finance (PM) is organising a Christmas party in its own ballroom, to which 436 employees are expected.
The expenses were measured at 2,500 forints per capita - around this money there is a buffet reception followed.
Since János Vere's tenure began, the employees of the PM celebrate in this way every year.
However, there will be not a real party mood - not only because of the official speech of the minister, but also because the celebration was scheduled for the afternoon.
The Ministry of Agriculture invites on January 20 to a New Year's reception, the ministry's Christmas party is cancelled this time.
You ask the one hundred guests to Vajdahunyad Castle in the Stadtpark ("Városliget"); according to information from the department, the expenses are dimensioned with 1,000 forints per guest.
As the press department reports, it will be a very simple stand-up reception, to which foreign diplomats accredited only in Hungary are invited.
The receptions, which are held each year, traditionally begin with a brief address by the minister and end with reluctant feasting.
As the corresponding press offices report on [origo] request, there will be neither a Christmas party at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs nor the Ministry of Education.
Asked about the costs of the Christmas party, no information could be given at the press department of the Ministry of the Environment.
In any case, more than 400 guests were invited to the celebration, which is held at the Kunstgewerbemuseum.
Increasing more forces mobilized for giant strikes
The League's trade unions and the Hungarian Trade Union Federation are forming their coordination committee on Friday for indefinitely
On Saturday, a large-scale protest meeting will be held, on Monday - on the day of the People's Vote on the Health Insurance Act - the work stoppage begins.
Strike-related, considerable traffic obstructions are expected, since rail traffic is almost completely discontinued, and the Budapest public transport companies (BKV) are also on strike until 13 o'clock.
Details on Monday's strike will be jointly announced on Friday afternoon by the League Trade Unions and the Trade Union Federation.
It will also set up a LEAGUE - MOSZ strike committee responsible for coordinating the actions on Monday.
On Saturday, a protest assembly will be held, and as of Monday, all organizations that have joined the strike will lay down work for an indefinitely if no agreement is reached with the government.
In other words, the end of the strike beginning on the day of the parliamentary vote on the health insurance law has not been defined.
If the National Council fails to adopt the health insurance law, the unions immediately withdraw the call to strike.
However, if the law is passed, the strike committees will meet to discuss how to proceed.
Due to the agenda of the National Council on Monday, it is even conceivable that it will only come to a vote on the health insurance law in the later evening hours, or even at midnight.
Due to the strikes beginning on Monday, one will have to face considerable traffic obstructions: the railway workers put down their work for an indefinitely time, in the morning the majority of public transport and more than half of the Volán companies stand still.
At the same time, there will be motorway barriers in many places in one direction.
Numerous trade unions and civil organizations have followed the call for the strike - the list of participants is continuously updated on the league website.
Some organizations announced strikes indefinitely, others are planning a temporary work stoppage.
Until Thursday afternoon, no agreement could yet be reached between the Hungarian Railway MÁV and the Free Trade Union of the railwaymen VDSZSZ on sufficient services, even though more and more people are travelling by train around the Christmas holidays.
According to previous reports, the BKV strikes between 7 a.m. and 9 p.m. from the start of operations until 1 p.m., the LIGA unions for electricity and heat energy put down work for four hours, and the teachers' union and the Democratic Teachers' Union have called for 2- and 2-8-hour strikes respectively.
The Hungarian Doctors’ Association is on an alternating strike at various times across the country, the EDDSZ, the democratic trade union of health and social care workers, a sub-organization of the SZEF, the Forum for cooperation of the trade unions, holds a two-hour warning strike.
The most important of the fourd points demanded by the league is that the government withdraws the draft health insurance reform and proposes a new conception for societal discourse.
The second demand concerns the gradual introduction of the new pension calculation model, which is due from next year.
Demand number three means that eligible professional groups will be given access to the early retirement system before 2010.
The fourth point is that the VDSZSZ, which belongs to the Union Unions, has not accepted the offer submitted by the Ministry of the Sub-Lines and is now calling for a new concept.
Political Capital: Hungarian Guard raises state risk
According to Political Capital's Risk Analysing Division, the activities of the Hungarian Guard, which was founded this August with the support of the "Movement for a Better Hungary," have increased the risk of Hungary and the region in several respects.
Among other things, in connection with the activities of the Hungarian Guard, the highest political risk poses the "intensification of the already existing ethnic tensions between the Gypsies and non-gypsy population in Hungary, which could even lead to violent ethnic clashes in the short term".
The main reason for this is the strategy of the Hungarian Guard, which presumably aims at the provocation of such a dispute, according to the research institute.
As reported, this is seen as a failure on the part of the political leaders: "Persons of public life have left the discussion of the Roma question to representatives of radical political groups, who have now built their political strategy on the Roma-Hungary opposition".
It is assumed that this would exacerbate the problem, make reasonable dialogue impossible, favor extremities, and increase the likelihood of violent confrontations.
The research institute also believes that the activities of the Hungarian Guard, not least thanks to the enormous attention on the part of the domestic and foreign media, "significantly damaged the image of Hungary abroad, in particular with regard to confidence in stability and democratic maturity".
We remember: Since the autumn of 2006, the reorganization of radical and extreme right-wing groups in Hungary has been continuously on the upswing, so that since the summer of 2007 several organizations that call themselves "national self-defense" have been founded.
"These groups have in common that they each define themselves as a civilian organization, while openly bragging about preparing their members for - sometimes armed - defensive tasks (disaster protection, national defense, order guard), citing the unsuitability of the police, the army, or the state itself," it said.
And further, according to that method, all of this is done, which seeks to camouflage various political aspirations, based on the political and partisan disenchantedness of the population as a bourgeois initiative.
The emphasis on the civil character and the form of the association therefore do not only serve to circumvent legal hurdles, which could arise in the course of registration in court - it is at the same time a political strategy.
According to the analysis, the activities of the Hungarian Guard also represent a risk-increasing factor in relations with neighbouring countries.
“In recent months, the Hungarian Guard has been cited in several neighboring states as a potential source of danger, according to some ultra-nationalist politicians of certain neighboring countries, in the emergence of the Hungarian Guard the confirmation of his own anti-Hungary policy.
This is all having an impact on the region's political stability," it said.
From the point of view of the research institute, the establishment of the Hungarian Guard is not an individual phenomenon in Central-Eastern Europe, so the political risk for the entire region has grown.
As reported, the activities of the Hungarian Guard are comparable to those of the Bulgarian National Guard, whose foundation was announced in August 2007 by the far-right Bulgarian National Union (BNU).
In the background of the formation of the Bulgarian Guard stood the Roma mass riots that have taken place in a district of Sofia (Krasna Poljana).
The BNU took the incidents as an occasion to establish the National Guarde, arguing that the organization's goal is "national protection."
In their opinion, in the past 17 years a veritable "gypsy terror" has been noticeable in Bulgaria, but the state has remained idly and indifferent - according to Political Capital.
And further: the initiative would have kicked off a fierce debate in the Bulgarian public.
As I said, the increased risk of ethnic conflicts is also evidenced by the fact that some Bulgarian Roma organisations have announced that they will establish their own guard for resistance purposes.
Since the activities of the Hungarian Guard so far show many similarities with those of the Bulgarian National Guard, it cannot be ruled out that the Hungarian Roma are also responding to the threats by organizing their own protection, according to the risk warning report of Political Capital.
Attila Juhász, director of research at Political Capital, said at the request of the news office: Risk analysis is one of the research institute's activities.
Risk analysis reports would be published annually and if risk-altering tendencies are discernible, public opinion is informed, as in the case by issuing the risk warning report.
Festivaties are not holidays everywhere
In Hungary, public holidays are strictly regulated by law, so that the practice of redisposing the working days around the holidays has now become established, but the coming year gives us fewer such days.
Also in the EU and in America, something else is meant by "holiday".
The Hungarian legal system distinguishes between state holidays and non-working days.
Work-free days are mostly church holidays, or are related to significant historical events.
We also celebrate New Year, for example: Both the last day of the old year and the first day of the new year are work-free days - the latter alone so that the New Year's Eve celebraters can rest.
Throughout the year we have a total of fourteen public holidays.
The state holidays are March 15, commemorating the revolution of 1848, and October 23, commemorating the 1956 Revolution.
On August 20, the foundation of the state, on 1 May the work is celebrated, on 1 and 2 November we commemorate our dead.
In addition, there are two holidays each for Easter, Pentecost, and Christmas.
Since our state, national or religious holidays rarely fall on a weekend, the practice of "working-day postponement" has proven itself in cases where we would only work one day before or after the holiday.
However, the price of the extended weekends is that we have to work very well on the Saturdays before, or go to school.
When this day, which falls before or after the holiday, is ultimately to be worked out, has long been stipulated by the Minister of Labour in its own ordinance.
Legislation applies to all employers and recipients, to public employees and civil servants alike.
Excluded are only companies and establishments in which the operation is continued without interruption, i.e. also on public holidays.
This year, the working regulations will only be somewhat confused by the holidays at the end of the year: From 24 December, life in this country will literally stop, as most for the days between Christmas and New Year's Eve take time compensation, or the specially cancelled holiday.
Next year, more public holidays fall into the middle of the week, or to the end of the week, so there will be fewer reschedulings at the working days than this year.
The calendar is "turned upside down" first around the day of work, so that people can stay at home for four days at a time.
Saturday April 26 will be a working day so that Friday, May 2 can be a day of rest.
The second rescheduling is related to the holiday on 23 October: on Saturday, October 18, you will have to pre-process the Friday, 24 October.
However, the end of the year will be completely dedicated to the holidays, since from December 24 everything lies and stops, even if New Year's Eve falls on a Wednesday.
Six deterrent trifles to annoy work colleagues
E-mails that we forget to send, voice messages that we never receive - harmless lies that we no longer notice: They bring "color" into everyday life in the workplace.
The only question is whether to get used to these small inconveniences, or you better face the challenge and try to avoid these misunderstant, for other annoying situations.
Tips.
"Sorry, I forgot to tell you you that someone from the marketing department called," says one colleague.
That you have missed a meeting?
At best you regret it and try to make up for it by perhaps calling the marketing department and remorsefully confessing that you forgot to align the news (that’s the less common method).
For the most part, the matter is dismissed with an apology.
Before you become entangled in awkward excuses, you should think about what you can give your colleagues truly unpleasant moments and try to avoid these situations consciously.
borrowed something that doesn't belong to you?
There’s little more annoying than you’re reaching for a document, office supplies, or the phone, but it’s not there.
Yesterday's stitching device was still lying on the desk - it's gone this morning?
Or is the lost ballpoint pen discovered on a colleague’s desk?
Without questions, you shouldn't borrow anything from your colleagues, because they are rightly upset when they realize that something has gone away.
So, don't put any envelopes or post-it out of the office in your pocket, because it could hit you yourself: none more there, if you urgently need it.
Gossip about the private lives of colleagues?
There is no effective means of stopping a gossip that has already been placed into the world.
Who with whom, what done?
Where and why?
If possible, one should not bind the information (or assumptions) about the private life of colleagues to everyone!
And if someone opens up and betrays us his innermost secrecy, we shouldn't abuse that trust!
It is best to remain silent like a tomb, rather thanking those who are not present, even if others also know about the problems of the colleague.
Reported sick for no reason?
If you call in the office to call, citing an excruciating headache, because you are currently feeling uncomfortable, or just want to rest, you should be aware of one thing about one thing: your own work, unsuspensive tasks, projects with fixed appointments must now be done by the colleagues instead of yourself.
Don’t play badly with your colleagues!
If you already know the day before that you won’t make it into the office the next day, or that something could come between, why you have to stay at home, you should report it in time, so that neither the colleagues nor the boss are unexpectedly confronted with absence.
Summing, giggles, making phone calls
One is just absorbed in one’s work, but is suddenly disturbed by the noisy giggles of his colleagues?
Unintentionally a telephone conversation overheard that shouldn't have been heard?
You shouldn't annoy the colleagues with hummings, singing, loud conversations (which one leads over their heads), trampling, constant scolding or nagging.
All of this can be very disturbing, even if they aren’t exactly engrossed in a task (writing, planning, organizing).
Are everyone else's fault?
If a project is not ready on time, you have forgotten to send an important letter or important message, you should not look for the guilt of your own forgetfulness with others!
If possible, you shouldn't talk about the chaos in the office or the noisy colleagues!
It’s best to admit your mistakes, and apologise if others were also affected by it!
The question of how to make up for the mistake, or catch up on what has been missed, is all too appropriate!
In the office - a feeling like at home?
Training pants and flogging: Comfortable for home, but has lost nothing in the office!
Not even then you should dress so casually, if the workplace would actually allow it!
Don't get too sloppy - you should respect both your colleagues and yourself to such an extent that you dress accordingly in the office - if desired, also pretty and elegant!
wanting to make it right for everyone - or how to become uninteresting for others
Stop and go traffic, the car in the middle of the morning traffic jam.
A strange melody resounds from the radio: the sound of a violin, paired with electronic effects.
I'll turn the volume higher.
The tempo changes, to the previous sounds comes the bass, and as a real sound jewel a female choir.
It's Zagar's "Wings of Love," which resounds on mr2.
For a long time, the local music scene didn’t exactly pamper us with high-quality electropop.
But now you even see the traffic jam differently when you listen to Zagar's new album.
The appearance of Yonderboi, many new and exciting productions from the domestic digital underground scene slowly contribute to a change in the overall picture, and even if the garage rock with its guitrary chatter is "in" again, the domestic electropop proves: There is something new under the sun.
Baláz's Zságer started in Pécs, made music even in the band Yonderboi, before - perhaps because an inn doesn't tolerate tworn two whistlers - began to tread his own path.
The breakthrough with his project (Zagar) and his music, which merges elements from jazz, electronic, and pop, but he only made it with the request to compose the soundtrack to a movie (e.g.
"Szezon" by Ferenc Török).
The formation, which had initially appeared in smaller clubs, only became known to the wider audience when the magazine "wan2" ranked their first album from 2002 among the top 50 domestic albums and Zagar, together with Yonderboi, named almost the only trendsetter that is also marketable in the West.
Undoubtedly, Zagar and his band were "trendy," but it didn't interest anyone whether they like to wear this brand.
The band has reacted as it should: with a new, even better album.
The now released new album titled "Cannot Walk Fly Instead" could open up neways for them, but the title already expresses the desire for continuity with the Pécs years.
Baláz’s Zságer was still on the road with another band.
The airy, flying allusion would also be fine, but in addition to the ethereal character of the music, the louder guitar - corresponding to the zeitgeist and the trends, the influences of rock'n'roll stand out.
With these songs, singer György Ligeti, who lives in England and works with the band "The Puzzle", introduced himself at the premiere at the Millenáris.
A song from the album has even made it to the Radioschlager - mr2 Petőfi broadcasting pop music from early to late to the listeners with the number "Wings of Love", which was recorded with the participation of six local singers ("Underground Divas"), has successively set the earworm.
Everything's all right (?).
The hvg.hu asked at Balázs Zságer.
hvg.hu: This new hit raises the question: To what extent is your music reserved for a certain layer?
Balázs Zságer: The term "shift music" doesn't exactly mean much to me.
In my opinion, there are two castes in pop music, not mainstream and underground, or alternative and pop, but commercial and honest.
Even the so-called alternative or underground music can be shallow and meaningless, but it can also only be produced for the sale, which turns it into commercial music.
Even a good pop number can come from an honest motive.
The readiness of the listener’s readiness also works according to this principle, independent of languages and national borders.
hvg.hu: Do you see a difference in the openness of the Hungarian and foreign audiences to your music?
Abroad, especially west of us, pop music has a greater tradition.
Not only teenagers or twens go to concerts, but also the older audience.
They follow the trends in pop music happening around them much more closely because they also consider them to be part of contemporary culture.
hvg.hu: What role does the audience play in your work?
Is it important who, where, how many listen to your music?
B. Zs.: Of course, it’s a good feeling to see that more and more are coming to our concerts.
We've never composed for the market, though.
With us, the music does not arise to serve a purpose, but for a specific reason.
The role of the audience is mostly noticeable at the concerts, where there is already a mutual interaction, but this in turn exists only in the intangible changes of the moment.
Nevertheless, I noticed one thing: If you want to please everyone, you are no longer interesting.
hvg.hu: how far would you like to follow Yonderboi's example?
He's on the road as a soloist and has built up his career rather in the West.
B. Zs.: We don’t really go his way, apart from the fact that you always try to interpret this into our music.
We see ourselves more as team players who trust in each other’s musical presence and allow them to unfold.
At first, of course, comparisons of our work were inescapable with Yonderboi's, as several of us come out of his ligament and took a different path.
hvg.hu: In the charts of mr2 Petőfi "Wings of Love" comes first.
Many have only heard the name Zagar in connection with this number.
Was the opening consciously staged with a slightly more popular style towards a wider audience?
B. Zs.: We had no plans to write a hit, which should then serve as a draught horse for the album.
This song is about more.
It was intended as the finale of the album, with which we wanted to resolve the sound orgy and the slightly more difficult themes.
hvg.hu: How did you choose the six singers?
Why exactly them, what was the guiding principle?
Z.S.: The basic idea was to make a gospel-like chorus.
Then came the idea to assemble the choir from gifted soloists of the local music scene.
This is how the six-member chorus was created - Sena, Edina Kutzora, Judie Jay, Bori Péterfy, Enikő Hodosi [Neo], Juci Németh - whom we then christened "Underground Divas".
hvg.hu: Did you want to have the song in the same way, or did the girls shape it?
For example: The number has been around for a long time in the instrumental version, but when the material for the album was finished, we thought that this number still "needs something."
The chord succession has formally demanded a singing voice.
I sung the melody on the same night, later they learned the girls.
It all came practically immediately and terrifyingly spontaneous.
I can remember, I was quite electrified at the time, but it also took the "divas".
With my voice alone, the song would never have
hvg.hu: But both on the record and at the concert you had the impression that the girls were in the background.
There's no solo part (apart from Judy Jay's short solo), they don't have a role of their own, they don't just sing together completely and aren't louder louder than the music, so their singing isn't just accompanied by the music.
They also stood at the back of the stage.
Is it intention?
B.Zs.: That they stood in the back was because of purely technical problems.
However, we deliberately didn’t want to give them any solo roles.
The number is also about forgetting one’s ego a little, forgetting who came from where and going, we simply allow these certain wings, the wings of love to raise us.
I'd like others to feel this euphoric state that we felt when the song originated.
hvg.hu: What was the most elaborate song?
B. Zs.: Actually none at all in the musical creative work, there have been more difficult tasks when mixing.
For example, "Dorian Gray's Dilemma", in which I play on a concert piano, or "Rock'n'Roll Goes To The Girls", where the Groove didn't want to work for a long time, but gradually everything came together, as with a puzzle.
hvg.hu: What's your favorite number from the new album?
B. Zs.: This changes constantly with the concerts and even now that I "let go" of the album.
"Prophet Is A Fool," "Escape From The Earth," and "Never The Same."
Fraudulent broker risked billions because of inferiority
Groundless scammer
His former employee Jerome Kerviel refers to the French banking giant Société Générale, owner of the local Komerční banka.
Because of him, it came by almost five billion euros.
However, it seems that Kerviel was only a great player who wanted to do the same to his colleagues, in addition was certainly not incapable.
In contrast to all the big banks, he recognized the crisis in the American mortgage market in time.
"He is mentally rather weak," could be heard from the mouths of his superiors who reported him to the police.
However, according to Kerviel's statements published by the French media, for example the daily Le Monde, he was a person who had made profits of €1.6 billion at the end of last year with his businesses.
Crisis not only in America
"All I wanted was to make money for my bank," he asserts in his statement.
In the summer, he managed to earn half a billion euros on the American crisis.
He had already seen the opportunity at the beginning of the year.
In February, there were already initial messages that so-called sub-prime mortgages, such as low-income customers or those who have had pay-of-the-bay problems before, would run into difficulties.
However, this crisis should not have an impact on the whole economy.
Kerviel didn't believe that and was right.
By the end of the year, he had raised his profit up to €1.6 billion.
"Even when I had earned the first half a billion, I didn't know how to explain it," Kerviel admits, saying he definitely didn't keep anything for himself.
During the whole interrogation, he admits that he forged documents so he wouldn't bent up.
He even managed to deceive his bosses, even if the Eurex stock exchange gradually became interested in his businesses.
Risk profit
Were he afraid to blow up?
I don't think so.
“I thought she satisfied the simple fact that I earned $1.6 billion for the bank.”
Where did he get the courage to risk so much?
He had to work out his position gradually.
He joined Société Générale in 2000 immediately after completing his studies at the University of Lyon.
His annual salary was about $51000.
After two years he was already an assistant to a broker and in 2005 he became a self-employed broker with an annual salary of 90000 dollars.
Nevertheless, he knewed that he would never earn as much as others.
“Already at my first conversation in 2005, I knew I was being watched.
Because of my education and also because of my professional and personal development," Kerviel explained to the investigators.
But soon after his entry came the first great success.
With Allianz's shares, he earned a lot by putting a half-billion euros on price losses of this title in the London bombings.
And that was the trigger - now he wanted more.
as long as you make a profit, we'll let you
"It's like a snowball," he told the policeman.
But the bosses supposedly knew about everything, or guessed it.
“As long as I made money, they closed their eyes.”
But then at the beginning of this year came the big blow.
In a single day, he lost one billion euros.
One weekend in mid-January, he wanted to make up for that.
When he came to work on Monday, he was no longer an employee of the bank.
And a few days later, the bank announced that it had lost five billion euros because of him.
And how did they come up to him?
Suspiciously, he went on holiday.
“I’ve only taken four days from the previous year.
A broker who doesn't take a vacation is one who doesn't want to look at him in the cards," Kerviel concluded.
Czech Republic has the chance to reach 365 billion
Czech ministers and representatives of the European Commission signed a total of nine development programmes yesterday, through which the Czech Republic can reach 14 billion euros (about 365 billion crowns) over the next few years.
It is about half of the sum that the Czech Republic can receive from Brussels in the years 2007 to 2013.
About 125 billion crowns come from seven regional development programs.
Czech companies will be able to be allocated about 100 billion crowns from the "Company and Innovation" programme.
Most money, about $150 billion, will flow into traffic expansion.
The transport programme is the largest development programme and comprises up to 22 percent of the funds that the Czech Republic can receive from the Fund.
This money increases according to the budget of the State Transport Infrastructure Fund, which will have 90.8 billion next year.
The "Company and Innovation" program is intended to serve entrepreneurs and companies to raise funds for innovative ventures.
As a result, the companies are to gain finances for the introduction of new technologies, for corporate research, but also for the introduction of more effective organizational methods.
In the previous monetary aquisition from EU funds, the Czech Republic is one of the least successful among the new candidate countries to date.
Nicaragua court upholds twenty-year prison sentence for ex-president
An appeals court in Nicaragua on Thursday upheld the twenty-year prison sentence for former President Arnold Alemán, to whom he had been sentenced in 2033 foold for €100 million (almost $1.8 billion).
Later, however, he had been released from prison and lived under house arrest on his hacienda near the Nicaraguan metropolis.
Whether the former head of state (1997 to 2002) of this Central American state will come back behind bars is currently unclear, according to the AP news agency.
According to Ileana Pérez's chairman of the Court of Appeal, it will now depend on the detention officers to let Alemán serve his sentence at home, or whether he must go to prison.
A court proved four years ago that Alemán had regularly shifted the government's money to accounts of foreign firms in Panama.
These companies were fictitious and the accounts actually belonged to his relatives and closest followers.
Some of this money eventually landed in a fund that Alemán had set up to finance his election campaign in 2001.
budget is through
Yes, it's done.
The state budget is approved.
What's special about this budget?
It is the first budget this government has drafted and that was adopted without the support of the opposition by the coalition majority.
The for the current year was still approved with the support of the ČSSD (Social Democrats) because Minister Sobotka (from this party) had presented it, and the ČSSD therefore could not afford to reject it.
In contrast to the lively debate before the vote on the no-confidence motion against the government, nothing dramatic happened this time.
The House of Representatives calmly endorsed this traditionally unnoticed, yet highly significant law.
That's, to a certain extent, paradoxical.
All governance begins and ends with the individual chapters and posts of the household, whether you like it or not.
The budget is an integral part of politics, but is concealed by the power struggle of the government and the opposition.
This is a more grateful issue for the media, and that's why they're paying more attention.
A dispute among politicians is much more interesting to the public than sober numbers.
The state budgets suffered from three weaknesses in the past Social-Democratic governments, which the budget for the year 2008 cannot eliminate at once:
The first is the high proportion of expenditures that are legally obligatory, which is not only about pensions, but also social spending decided by the "tacity" coalition of ČSSD and KSČM (communists) in the last year before the elections.
The second is deficient fiscal policy as a result of the first weak point.
The third restriction is the deficits of public finances, i.e. the budget deficiencies of the municipalities.
However, the biggest risk is not the government budget deficit.
This budget is publicly and reasonably under scrutiny.
However, the budget deficit of the municipalities is a true time bomb.
Many mayors, in addition to income from taxes, levies and bank loans, often issued unconsidered debt securities, through which they indebted their town halls in excess.
In recent years, they have managed to pay off this debt through mass sales of shares of the energy and other utilities that they had received from the state in the course of privatization.
Now this source has already dried up.
As far as we adhere only to official and verifiable facts, we find that:
Our economy, like the rest of the world, has been undergoing a phase of growth for several years, which the ČSSD boasts without ceasing.
This represented a once-in-a-lifetime opportunity for their governments to put public finances in order.
Instead, however, these governments indebted the state even more.
Other countries, apart from a few exceptions such as Hungary, Albania, Pakistan or Egypt, have managed much more sensibly than our republic.
This is a huge disgrace that is mainly attributable to former governments.
We are among those countries that, on a global scale, are least at risk of poverty.
Therefore, no massive social programs are needed, which have unfortunately been inflated quite superfluously in recent years on a gigantic scale.
Even if it has succeeded in removing part of the nonsensical populist spending passed this year, it is clear that in the near future there will be a postponement of the retirement age and a pension reform, then virtually any policy in this country will end, and, no matter who is in power, it will remain nothing but mere administration.
It's only slightly exaggerated to say it's that then ruling politicians become civil servants who mechanically allocate most of the tax revenue to pensions and social spending.
Therefore, this year's budget cannot be a revolution, but can only put a stop to the displacement of normal expenditure through compulsory spending.
The deficit of €70 billion planned for 2008 would be superfluous if previous governments hadn’t spent in the last decade.
If we don’t want that it really happens that ruling politicians become pure administrative officials, then we have to be patient and this not too strong coalition government, which, however, is aware that the state can only spend as much as it is able to take in taxes and fees, want calm in the preparation of further reform laws.
Only these make it possible to progressively enforce a balanced state budget.
Jiří Krejčí
Banks want to deal with crisis
The U.S. Federal Reserve, the European Central Bank and the central banks of Britain, Switzerland and Canada have decided to fight together against the crisis in the financial markets.
More than $100 billion through auctions will enter the currency markets.
At these auctions, the banks are auctioning interest rates at which they are willing to lend money to each other.
For example, the American Fed sends $40 billion at auction, and another $24 is making it loose for the European and Swiss central bank.
It is the first time after the attacks of September 11 that the central banks of the individual states agree on such a coordinated approach to prevent or mitigate the consequences of a financial crisis.
"This step is intended to eliminate the greatest difficulties of the economy, i.e. the increasing lack of freely available capital that the banks can lend," wrote in a report for his clients Bernard Baumohl of the consulting firm Economic Outlook Group.
"Coordinated action by central banks has reduced the likelihood of a recession in the U.S. from 65% to about 50%," Baumohl added in his report.
Banks have already lost $60 billion because of unserved loans, especially in the United States.
Analysts are expectinging further billion-dollar losses.
"The central banks must have been genuinely afraid of what can still be achieved when decided on this approach," Kieran Davies, chief economist of ABN Amro said.
"It looks like the situation is really bad," he continued.
The central banks have the fear that if the banks themselves have heavy access to bonds, loans will also become more expensive for their customers.
That would reduce consumption and plunge the American economy into a recession, possibly with an impact on economic growth around the world.
The central banks have decided, after failing to lower the so-called LIBOR, the London interbank interest rate used in lending money between banks.
This means that the banks were constantly afraid to borrow money from each other, even though the central banks of the U.S., Canada and Britain had lowered their interest rates several times during the fall.
"This will have the same effect as a further reduction in interest rates," Greg Salvaggio of Tempus Consulting told Reuters.
Some analysts are of the view that this move by central banks contributes to heating up the crisis because it does not solve the fundamental problem, namely the fall of the real estate market in the USA.
Klaus: Ecologists' debates are unbelievable
Global climate changes are reportedly harming developing countries the most.
President V�áclav Klaus again commented on the problem of global warming.
Criticism now referred to the U.N. international conference on climate climate in Bali.
In Vienna, where he presented his book "A blue, not a green planet," he pointed out that the ecologists' debates were unbelievable.
According to Klaus, not only the structure of the conference is unacceptable, which forms a strange mixture of government officials and those of non-governmental organizations, but also the fundamental difference between the rhetoric that accompanies it and reality.
"The debate there is unstructured, it's a word firework that's just a replacement for a real solution to current problems," Klaus said.
The Czech president emphasized at the presentation of his book that his topic was very important for him, and that he was therefore so committed to it.
He also expressed his satisfaction that his book appears in German translation because "I often encounter caricatures of my views."
Now interested parties could read his attitude undistorted.
Klaus recalled that he is not a climatologist, but in his opinion he had sufficient prerequisites to write such a book.
After all, he is an academic, able to understand and assess technical treatises, has experience with statistics and is also an economist.
As the main statement of the book, he described the thesis that the climate is constantly changing and that its changes are therefore normal, that there is no consistent opinion among the scientists on global warming, that the consequences of the current changes are not too great and serious and that the efforts to influence the development of the climate are superfluous, ineffective and unacceptably complex.
In addition, as someone who has experiences with totalitarian systems, he considers it unacceptable to impose unproven views on others, he sees this as a dangerous restriction of freedom.
Asked questions from attendees, he repeatedly stated that he was not opposed "to a reasonable environmental protection, but against militant eco-activists, who take no account of the relationship between effort and yield."
Klaus had already discussed the same topic in Vienna two months ago at the Hayek Institute.
600000 people failed to exchange their driver's license
600000 motorists missed the deadline to exchange their old driver's license, whose validity expires at the end of the year.
Today is the last day where you can apply for the exchange so that the authorities manage to issue a new driver's license by the end of the year.
However, the ministry does not fear that hundreds of thousands of drivers will take to the roads without a valid driving license.
According to the authorities, mainly those have not exchanged their driver's license who no longer want to drive.
While small towns are reporting a sharp drop in interest in driving licence exchanges, the capital's offices are being showered with applications, and interest in the exchange was still growing this week.
The city administration handles around 2000 customers every day.
You have to queue for about half an hour.
In Prague, about 140000 drivers have not yet exchanged their driver's license.
The issuance of a new driver's license lasts 20 days.
But it is possible to apply for the issuance with a shortened five-day deadline.
In this case, however, the driver will have to pay 500 crowns.
Motorists who do not manage the prescribed exchange until the end of the year and drive off without a valid driver's license face a fine from January.
The offender pays a maximum of 2000 crowns.
The police, however, can also have it with an admonition during the transgression.
In the event of transgression, the penalty can rise to 2500 kronor.
According to previous information, even those can be imposed by the municipality, who does not drive at all.
He could, it was said, threaten a penalty of even 30000 crowns.
The ministry has already called on the offices to exercise mildness and at most impose a penalty of 1000 crowns.
According to the spokesman of the Ministry of transport, Karel Hanzelka, however, does not threaten a thousand-crown penalty.
The driver’s license is a document in which everyone can decide whether they want to own it or not.
It is not an identity card that everyone must have, namely a valid one.
People who don't want to drive anymore should surrender their driver's license.
It is sufficient to take him to the competent municipal office or send him there by registered letter.
At the end of the year, driving licences issued between 1964 and 1993 become invalid.
However, motorists will also have to gradually exchange other types of driver's licenses.
People with driving licences from the years 1994 to 2000 still have three years for the exchange.
The validity of driver's licenses issued from 2001 to 2004 ends in December 2013.
According to Hanzelka, there are about 2.6 million other driver's licenses.
Court lowers penalties for detectives allied with Berdych
Former police officers Josef Opava and Petr Koňařík, who are accused of having made common cause with David Berdych's gang, received 13 and 10 years of imprisonment respectively.
In a final judgment, the Prague Higher Regional Court ruled, which reduced their sentence by one or two years.
Originally, the detectives of the "Office for the Fight Against Organized Crime" threatened 14 and 12 years, respectively.
Against today's verdict, there is no possibility of appeal, only revision at the Supreme Court is possible.
Berdych himself is also charged in this matter, but he is initially facing the first-instance verdict.
At other trials, however, he had already received sentences from a total of 16 years.
He cannot be condemned to more.
A district court pronounced in January 19 people mainly for involvement in robberies, the Higher Regional Court today heard the appeal of 16 of them.
From the beginning, most of the defendants denied the indictment on the grounds that it was a constructed case.
According to the Central Bohemian District Court, relatives of the police in consultation with Berdych criminals handed over information from sources of the police, covered various robberies and helped members of the gang to evade prosecution.
The gang handed over 30 percent of the prey to Opava and Koňařík.
The indictment is about crimes committed by the gang from 1999 to 2001.
In particular, robberies whose victims often suffered serious injuries, but also for presumptuousness and illicit possession of weapons.
During house searches, the police discovered illegal pistols, ammunition with a steel core, a laser riflescope and a submachine gun.
According to the indictment, the gang has enriched itself by many millions of crowns through their robberies.
In addition to almost 200 men in this proceedings, courts recognized Berdych and a number of others for further robberies as guilty.
Other men from Berdych's peripherience have to answer in another trial, further cases have not yet come to court.
Tomáš Půta and Maroš Šulej, who are in Irish detention and are awaiting the final decision on their extradition for prosecution in the Czech Republic, have so far escaped the main hearing.
Children swapped - Invoice: 12 million
For the exchange of children in a maternity hospital, the lawyer of the aggrieved parents, Igor Veleba, wants to demand 12 million kronor from the hospital in Třebíč 12 million crowns plus court costs.
For each of the mothers three million each, for the fathers two million each and one million each for the children.
The hospital offers a maximum of 200000 for each pair of parents.
The legal representative of the hospital, Ondřej Dostál, therefore completely disagrees with Veleba's proposal.
"We reject such a proposal, it is many times too high," Dostál said after today's trial.
"However, in order to form an opinion on this counter-proposal, we need the expert opinion that had the other side prepared.
We'll determine if it's meaningful at all.
However, we insist on the view that this together should be in the hundred-thousander range for everyone," Dostál emphasized.
According to him, the amount should move between 100000 and 200000 crowns for each of the four parents.
In the beginning, the Jabloňov family wanted a million crowns for every month it had not raised their biological daughter.
For Veleba served as the basis for compensation negotiations an expert opinion, which the University Hospital in Prague Motol had worked out for him.
According to experts in psychology and psychiatry, the parents of the children suffered psychological damage and stress symptoms in varying strength and depth.
The men tolerated the stress situation better, even if they faced accusations that they could have prevented the confusion of newborns.
The women, in turn, cope worse with the after-effects of the trauma experienced, as well as with what is going on right now, Veleba summarized the content.
Veronika and Nikola swapped nurses of the maternity hospital in Třebíč on 9 December last year.
Last week the children returned to their biological parents - Nikola to the Čermáks in Přibyslavice, and Veronika to Libor Brož and Jaroslava Trojanov.
At the end of October, the district captain of the Vysočina (Böhmian-Moravian Height) handed over 100000 crowns to each family as a replacement for expenses incurred by the fault of the hospital in Třebíč, which is subject to the district.
All hospitals in the Czech Republic are covered by liability insurance by law.
If in doubt, they have to resort to a reserve fund, only exceptionally can they apply for assistance from the institution.
The courts have so far awarded comparatively low amounts of damages to those affected, the amounts have been determined by tables and moved in the range of hundreds of thousands, exceptionally of millions.
Polívka came around his kingdom Wallachia
Bolek Polívka lost the dispute over the "Kingdom of Wallachia".
According to the judge at the district court in Ostrava, he was the first to use the designation "Walachian King", but the project had built a team around Tomáš Harabiš in cooperation with the actor.
Polívka did not participate in the trial today.
According to his lawyer, however, the matter is probably not finished and his client will probably appeal.
"The dispute is that Mr. Harabiš and his team want to participate parasitically in Bolek Polívka's popularity," Polívka's lawyer said.
Everyone introduced themselves at the words "Walach's Kingdom" this actor and comedian.
Harabiš is of the same opinion.
“We’ve had almost ten years of work.
raising money and concepts of activities," he said.
According to him, the occasion is a dispute over money.
“After years, they have noticed that the project is successful and that they could also have some of it.
They want to gain parasitically in our work," he continued.
Lucrative business
Polívka used the designation "Walakh king" for the first time in one of his television broadcasts.
In 1993 he was crowneded by the actor Radoslav Brzobohatý.
At the time, however, this was not related to the intention of establishing a fictitious kingdom.
A few years later, Harabiš came up with the idea of issuing Wallach passports.
From an originally fun action, a business and an entrepreneurial activity crystallized, to which Harabiš then joined the foundation of the company "Walachian Kingdom".
In 1997 Polívka agreed to the use of his name and his signature in the passports and even offered their owners discounts when visiting his farm and theatre performances.
Who's going to sit on the throne?
The judge also stated today that Harabiš used only the mere idea that Polívka had made public for his entrepreneurial intentions in the "Manege" show.
"Polívka's popularity has certainly helped the Wallach Kingdom's project," the judge said.
However, she added that the soul of the Harabiš project and his team were devoted to him intensively.
The cooperation of today’s disintegrated parties lasted until 2000, when Harabiš organized the official coronation for Polívka in Vsetín.
Since then, however, disputes between Polívka and Harabiš began to pile up, culminatinating "King Boleslav's I elected until Saint-Neverlein's Day".
Harabiš's Jarmila Šuláková as Queen's mother then took up the leadership role.
In 2002 Polívka filed a lawsuit against Harabiš and his company, which the court dismissed today.
Clarity in the dispute could bring the Harabiš-prepared competition for the throne and half a princess.
A new king was to emerge from this.
He did not want to reveal details and an appointment yet.
“We don’t want to rush anything.
It was supposed to be something like the king's parade in Wallachia.
The king should be elected for a certain period of time.
Mr Polívka, too, can quietly sign up," Harabiš added.
Test Peugeot 207 SW 1.6 VTi
To tell you the truth, I was never a special fan of the Peugeot 206.
Its driving characteristics weren't worth much, the quality of the gearshift, steering and processing could be described as rather below average.
But now his successor, the Peugeot 207, is on the market, and it is much better.
The combination with the designation SW is still a bit further, and I only have to revise my opinion.
When I had tested the classic hatchback of the 207, for example, I had to complain about the too little headroom on the back seats.
These complaints no longer exist, because the body of the station wagon is designed in such a way that not even two-meter men will suffer.
Fast further
I only go through the interior for a short time, so as not to repeat myself too much.
It is a quite successful design, where I only have objections because of the meagre central tunnel.
I’d welcome a little more design, where the car usually makes such an elegant impression.
The seats are comfortable, and over are the days when we may have complained about too short seats or headrests.
The side support has also improved significantly compared to the 206.
As far as space is concerned, you can also feel the enlarged dimensions of the body here.
In the combination, the already mentioned lack of headroom is also eliminated.
Suitcase into the trunk
However, the main thing about this car is the hold.
I have to describe it (also because of the hinged rear window) as very easy to load and space-saving.
The cavities are equipped with straps, which are an ideal compromise.
They don't bother, allow themselves to be pulled apart and also hold on to heavier objects.
But let's talk about the loading volume.
According to the table, it is 428 liters in the basic equipment, which is a rather average value compared to the competition.
However, however, compared to the best-selling vehicle of this class, the Škoda Fabia Combi.
If we compare the 207 SW with the new generation of the product from Mladá Boleslav, we find that the Peugeot is left behind by a clearly noticeable 52 liters.
For a holiday abroad or when loading a stroller, this value is already critical.
But in order to highlight the positiva, I still have to mention the very flat loading space or the very low loading edge after folding the rear seats.
120 hp in four rows
Our test car was equipped with a four-cylinder petrol engine with a displacement of 1.6 liters and an output of 88 kWp.
And honestly, that's what a 1600-cubic engine should look like, driving a family hatchback.
He's quiet, has low consumption and pulls away well.
If I compare him to his counterpart at Škoda, which sometimes looks as if his exhaust was clogged with a rag, then I can only be thrilled with him.
The price list speaks volumes
The price of this car isn't exactly popular.
However, we need to remember that we tested a well-equipped car with a spacious interior and a very good engine.
The basic price of the car with the 1400 gasoline engine in the amount of 329900 crowns is stilling.
With our engine, it costs from 410900 crowns, which is already quite a lot.
The premium equipment of the test car beats once again with 80000 kronor in addition to beech.
However, we had head airbags, automatic air conditioning, automatic windscreen wiper release, electric windows at the rear and...
For example, the car costs a total of 531900 crowns.
That's a lot, but calculate the additional equipment of a Fabia together and you'll arrive at a very similar sum.
All that remains is to decide which brand you want to choose.
Integration of Slovak pupils in British schools fails
In British schools, the number of children from countries in Eastern Europe is increasing, which raises fears among locals about the further development of the local education system.
So far, the integration of Eastern Europeans has encountered a number of obstacles.
British schools even detect cases where disputes between immigrants and the children of the original inhabitants increase in violence.
The problem with the integration of children from Eastern Europe into British schools aptly illustrates the case of North Kent, where clashes between locals and Slovak immigrants are gradually increasing.
Disputes, harassments and scrambles among pupils have transferred to relationships between parents.
The situation has already developed to such an extent that police in Chatham arrested a Slovakian woman who had beaten a ten-year-old boy with an iron bar.
The accused defended herself by saying that the boy had insulted her with racist insults.
Police also had to intervene on a Slovakian immigrant after an attack by two underage girls.
The girls had allegedly made racist remarks towards the man and pelted him with stones.
“When we went home, a boy climbed on a bushhouse and spat on us.
When I told him to stop, he threw apples and pears at us and then exposed his buttocks," a mother of Slovakian pupils told The Times another unpleasant incident.
Complaints come from both sides.
One of the locals even described the dispute with the immigrants from Eastern Europe as a struggle for survival.
The mentioned cases testify that the mood between locals and immigrants at British schools is becominging increasingly tense.
In the United Kingdom, 70000 Slovaks are registered to come here looking for a job.
About 30000 Slovak pupils attend British schools.
Cuba - the most beautiful island in the Caribbean
To visitors, it is truly an island of freedom.
Whatever you expect from your holiday, that will come true if you only choose the right thing.
Golden and white beaches, high waves or a sea that is quiet like a pond.
Palm trees and all-inclusive with waiters offering pizza, ice cream and beguiling cocktails on the beach.
Kitesurfing, golf and roulette.
On the islands of the Caribbean you will find this to an abundant extent.
However, you will only find much more on an island, and this island is Cuba, the largest and most beautiful of the Greater Antilles.
At the time of the Spaniards, Cuba was their bastion for the conquest of America.
Columbus called it the most beautiful place a human eye has ever seen.
Havana once became a warehouse or transit camp of treasures the Spaniards had robbed in America and then transported them to Europe.
And then came the American era.
The Hotel Nacional originated at the very end of the 20th century.
One of the bosses of the American mafia, Lucky Luciano, built, because everything that was considered a crime in the U.S. (especially during Prohibition), was gladly seen and supported in Cuba - drugs, gambling, prostitution.
Among his guests were, among others.
Winston Churchill, Ava Gardner, Frank Sinatra.
In Havana, high-rise buildings grew, over the wide boulevards drove cars, which were among the cult symbols of the 1950s of the last century.
Ernest Hemingway bought an estate in San Francisco de Paula, near Havana, and in Cojímar he had his yacht lying.
On the walls of Bodeguita del Medio, stars of show business and politicians from all over America left their signatures.
And today?
On the walls of Havanna's Bodeguita you too can leave your autograph.
Cocktails can be mixed at the bar at Hotel Nacional.
And the cult cars of the fifties drive around Havana to this day, because the once-imported Schigulis and Fiats have long since fallen apart in the salty and humid air.
May Havana, especially its oldest part, have come down considerably, nowhere can you find so many wonderful colonial sights in the periquence as right here.
Other places are being cleaned out right now, for example Trinidad.
In view of the fact that tourism brings much-neededed money, there has been a green light for this development.
In the last fifteen years, new hotels have not only emerged in Havana, but also in Varadero, on the coast of Holguín province and also on islets such as Cayo Coco, Cayo Guillermo and others.
Cuba still offers a quirk that is unique in the world and especially attracts railway lovers.
The sugar cane from remote plantations brought steam- and diesel-powered narrow-gauge railways to the sugar factories.
Their fleet has not been renewed for decades, and so you can see cute and fully functioning locomotives, whose peers have long been in the museum elsewhere.
Excursion destinations
The most important places do not leave out the majority of Cuba's visitors.
So you don't have to write in detail about Varadero or Havana.
All that remains to be mentioned is that there is a museum in Hemingway's already mentioned estate, whose visit the one who likes this writer should not be missed.
The Viñales Valley in the west of the island is one of Cuba's most picturesque corners.
The lush vegetation grows on lime soil.
The mogotas, lime mounds and cliffs, protrude steeply from the bottom of the valley, in their cavities accumulate fertile soil, through which even weathered rock areas are covered by dense vegetation.
This valley is where perhaps the best Cuban tobacco is grown.
In its final form you can meet him if you visit one of Havanna's cigar factories.
Possibly it also seems interesting to some that the Chilean episode of the television series "Major Zeman" was filmed there.
Trinidad
An ancient city that was founded shortly after Spaniards colonized the island.
Glory and wealth brought her the trade of slaves and sugar.
The wealth impersonated itself in the beautiful beauty of the small town.
The Spanish colonial architecture, strongly influenced by the later French settlers, has hardly changed for four hundred years.
Trinidad, situated between the sea and the Escambray mountains, is such a secluded place that there was still no road there in the fifties of the last century that would have connected the area to the rest of the country.
This isolation helped make it go unnoticed.
Nothing was built there, but that's why nothing was demolished, everyone was glad that he had a roof over his head.
If you have more than an afternoon for Trinidad, it is worthwhile to look at interesting places in the area, such as Playa Ancón beach or the "Valley of Sugar Factories".
In the evening you should go to the Casa de la Música, which revives after the darkness, and where the music is not silent until the morning.
Baracoa
This little town in the very east of Cuba is described by some as the most beautiful of the island.
Certainly this view is also influenced by the way there.
Both roads leading from Guantánamo have to overcome tall and rugged mountains and run through a landscape that is barely touched by human intervention.
This eastern outpost of Cuba was included in its World Heritage directory thanks to the unspoiled nature.
There the best chocolate is made, in the church one shows the cross of Columbus (in reality it was created about a hundred years after the voyages of Columbus), and from the hill, where the former castle, the current Hotel El Castillo stands, several table mountains can be seen, of which El Yunque is the best-known.
About the coffee, the rum, the cigars, the Sierra Maestra Mountains, the Bay of Pigs and the island of Isla de Pinos, about the Tropicana cabaret, the shrine of the Cuban patroness Caridad del Cobre, about the Santeria, an African religion to which the descendants of slaves profess, you could write many pages.
Yet, as they say: better to look with your own eyes.
Allies in Afghanistan are passive, Americans claim.
Too few soldiers and the lack of necessary equipment, according to the United States influences the course of the mission in Afghanistan.
According to Defense Secretary Robert Gates, NATO should draft a concrete plan for the next few years.
The mission in Afghanistan did not proceed according to their ideas, the representatives of the countries that stationed units in the south of the country agreed in Edinburgh, Scotland.
In addition to growing resistance from the Taliban, the eight participating states are also discussing further coordination plans among the allies.
While the United States, through its Defense Secretary Robert Gates, called on NATO units to more activity, Britain sees priority in boosting the Afghan government's authority and intensifying development aid.
“In Afghanistan, a forward development is noticeable, but military power can only be part of the solution.
Another part is to allow residents to start a further development of the economy and security themselves," Britain's Defence Secretary Des Browne tells the BBC.
Ninety percent
The activity of Afghan insurgents has reached its highest level in five years, according to the American Army.
The number of acts of violence has risen by 27 percent year-on-year, in the southern province of Helmand even by sixty percent.
NATO countries have deployed about 40000 troops in Afghanistan, about a quarter of them in the southern territory.
According to the United States, however, the allies have reached an absolute limit in terms of organization and equipment of the units.
Specifically, they are calling for more helicopters, soldiers and experts to train the Afghan units.
"We're going to have to think seriously about the concrete goals for the nextension and the way we can achieve them," Gates said.
NATO's secretary-general, Jaap de Hoop, expressed similar sentiments today.
“I’m not pleased with what we have available on land and in the air in Afghanistan.
We have about ninety percent of the soldiers and equipment we need, and that's too little," De Hoop told Reuters at the conclusion of three-day talks with the Japanese government in Tokyo.
Who's Passive
There has also been arguing among the countries involved in recent months over whether allies in Afghanistan will do their best.
Germany, for example, has been criticized because of passivity.
“The Germans are relatively numerously represented in Afghanistan.
However, they are stationed in areas where there is only a minimum of violence and the fighting requirements for them are not as high as for us in the southern and eastern part of the country," Gates said.
The British tabloid "Sun" this week referred to the German soldiers as sluggards.
"Afghanian non-twear revealed: the Germans make games while our boys are fighting," the "Sun" wrote in a post supplemented by photos of German soldiers playing table tennis.
The German Bildzeitung went over to the counter-attack and printed a picture of a British unit at a rugby competition in the southern part of Afghanistan.
Government debt will skyrocket to a trillion in 2009
Each Czech accounts for a national debt of 84000 kronor.
Total debt rose to 842.7 billion crowns by the end of September.
The government had planned to default this year with 159.2 billion cones.
In early December, however, thanks to the better development of state fiscal policy, the ministry reduced the plan to 152.8 billion.
The Czech Republic's national debt rose to 842.7 billion crowns by the end of September.
Within the first nine months of this year, they increased by 40.2 billion.
Since June, however, they have fallen by 13.4 billion.
The Ministry of Finance thus confirmed his information about the national debt from the beginning of October.
Each Czech accounts for 84000 kronor.
Over the entire past year, the state accounted for more than 111 billion crowns.
“Until now, it seems that, thanks to the lower deficit for the coming year, we may not exceed the limit of one trillion kronor until 2009.
Next year, the debt should move between 900 billion and a trillion crowns," David Marek, an analyst at Patria Finance, told the iHNed.cz server.
Vladimír Pikora, an analyst of Next Finance, sees it similarly: "We could get to exceed one trillion in the course of 2009.
Exactly, depends on the next budget we don't know yet.
If it should become apparent that the situation on the political stage is changing and politicians should be willing to save more, that could last until 2010.
But I don't assume such a change," he remarked.
Debt growing
The national debt stems from the debts of the state government and comes above all from the accumulation of deficits of the state budget.
The national debt is financed by Treasury notes, government bonds, direct bonds or, for example, by bonds of the European Investment Bank.
Domestic public debt has now risen to 719.4 billion crowns this year.
The state's external debt, meanwhile, increased by 1.7 billion crowns to 123.3 billion.
The so-called marketable sovereign debt reached 803.3 billion crowns at the end of September and fell by 13.7 billion since June.
The average maturity of the national debt increased to 6.5 years.
“Total debt increased 13 percent over the year from the first to the third quarter, which is really a lot given how well the economy is doing.
It is interesting that the growth is mainly in the case of bonds, where the increase is 20 percent.
It's bad that debts are rising, but on the other hand, this shows that you can do something even before the pension reform comes," Pikora commented on the data.
Better than expected
It is clear from the financing strategy and the handling of the national debt for 2007 that the government had planned for this year to take in 159.2 billion crowns.
In early December, however, the ministry reduced the plan to 152.8 billion kronor thanks to the better development of state fiscal policy.
This amount stems mainly from the state budget deficit, which will obviously reach 76 billion kronor this year instead of the estimated 91.3 billion.
In addition, it involves a maximum budget of 13.3 billion kronor for the consolidation agency, a tens of billioons to pay pensions, the financing of government bonds for €43 billion and installments for bonds at the European Investment Bank of 1.8 billion.
For giving a name to a teddy bear, she was presented to a court in Khartoum today.
According to her lawyer, the British teacher, who had been charged with insulting Islam in Sudan, was sentenceded to 15 days imprisonment and subsequent expulsion by a court in Khartoum on Thursday evening.
The British woman was accused of allowing six- to seven-year-old students to give a teddy bear the name Mahomet, although any depiction of the Islamic prophet is prohibited.
She deserved to be whipped.
After parents of pupils complained several times to Sudan's Ministry of Culture, the 54-year-old elementary school teacher, who worked at Khartoum's Unity High School, was apprehended at her home on Sunday and, under Article 125 of the Sudanese Penal Code, of insulting Islam.
In fact, this matter begins in September, when the teacher asks her six- to seven-year-old pupils to pick out a name for the class teddy.
"The children are proposing eight names, including Hassan, Abdullah and Mohammed," reports headmaster Robert Boules.
After all, when the class votes, 20 out of 23 children choose Mohammed as their favorite first name.
Certainly, Muhammad is the name of the Islamic prophet, but above all also a very common name in Sudan.
Subsequently, the children have created a book for the class, with the photo of the bear on it and the caption "My name is Mohammed".
The bear himself did not carry a sign with this name, but the problem is quite simply that the depiction of the Islamic prophet is seen as an insult to Islam.
One of Gillian Gibbon’s Moslemian colleagues, who teaches at the same school in Sudan, explained that he knew her well, and she would never have done anything with which she could get too close to a religious belief, “I only regret that she has asked the children to vote for a name,” in his opinion it is only an “innocent mistake.”
An opinion shared by numerous students of the teacher, who describe them as “very attentive to their pupils” and a “person who never talks about religion in the class”.
Keep in mind that the first name Mohammed is at the same time the second most commonly given second name in Great Britain, for which 14 different spellings exist in English.
This first name, often chosen by Muslim families, is considered a kind of reverent for the prophet (it is often given to the firstborn of a family).
But what are the rules regarding the use of that first name?
Can you give it to a toy, for example?
This, of course, is a matter of interpretation.
On Wednesday, no agreement was reached at the last negotiating meeting in Vienna on the future status of the province.
At the end of failure
The Serbs and Albanians from Kosovo ended a sixth and final discussion session Wednesday morning in Baden, Austria, without being able to overcome their controversies about the future status of the separatist province.
"It is unfortunate that the parties could not come to any agreement on the status," European mediator Wolfgang Ischinger explained at a press conference in Vienna.
Emphasizing that peace in the region is at stake, its American counterpart Frank Wilsner urged Belgrade and Pristina to fulfill their commitments to maintain peace, and to continue the talks beyond the end of the negotiations.
“No acts of violence are expected in the immediate future, but we are at the beginning of a very difficult time,” he added, suggesting that there are clearly tensions.
End of mediations on December 10
The troika's mediators, which unites the European Union, the U.S. and Russia, will meet one last time in Serbia and Kosovo on Monday.
The future of 90% by Albanian-speaking province could become clearer if Ischinger, Wilsner and Russian mediator Alexander Botsane-Khartchenko submit their report to the U.N. Security Council by December 10.
The talks of Baden, near Vienna, formed the sixth and last negotiating session since the Troika received an order from the UN last August for a new mediation.
Americans and Europeans expect that their mediation mandate will end on December 10.
The Russians, who had already blocked the approval of Kosovo's independence at the Security Council, insist on a continuation of negotiations beyond that end date.
“Russia was and will remain advocate of a negotiated solution, an amicable solution,” Botsane-Khartchenko said.
Unilateral declaration of independence?
After Kosovo has been under the administration of the UN for eight years, Western countries consider independence under supervision by the EU to be the only solution with a future.
Pristina is ready to unilaterally announce them if the Security Council should not decide in their favor.
Belgrade insists on stronger autonomy
“Unfortunately, no agreement could be reached,” said Fatmir Sejdiu, the president of Kosovo’s autonomous authority at the hotel in Baden, where these “negotiations of the last chance” have been held since Monday.
Hashim Thaci, former leader of the Kosovo rebellion and future prime minister of the province shared his regret.
However, he added that Belgrade’s proposed plan for autonomy “belonged to a past that led to murders and deportations.”
Domino effect
Serbian President Boris Tradic, for his part, was of the opinion that a compromise should always be sought, and warned the Albanian majority from Kosovo of the consequences of a unilateral declaration of independence by the province.
Belgrade thinks that it is particularly a "domino effect" in a region weakened by the wars of independence of the 1990s.
"For Serbia, this doesn't mean a compromise would be impossible," he said.
On Tuesday, the Serbian delegation insisted that any decision would be made solely and solely in the Security Council, where its Russian allies have veto power.
Kosovo's parliament will have the final say
"Only a decision from within the ranks of the Security Council is acceptable to Serbia," Serbian Prime Minister Vojislav Kostunica said.
These negotiations lasted two years.
They have to end as they began: in the Security Council.
Fatmir Sejdiu replied to the journalists that “the parliament of Kosovo will have the final say in terms of status,” adding that any unilateral declaration of independence on the part of the Albanian-speaking people would take place “in coordination” with Western countries.
Bush elicits agreement between Israelis and Palestinians
At the opening of the international peace conference, which had gathered Tuesday under his leadership in Annapolis, Maryland, President Bush announced that Israelis and Palestinians had finally agreed on a joint declaration.
He further stated that Israeli Prime Minister Ehud Olmert and Palestinian President Mahmoud Abbas had agreed to immediately begin negotiations to find a definitive settlement of the 60-year-old disputes between their two peoples.
In the presence of the two men, Bush promised that he would do everything he could to help them make a “Peacement Treaty” before the end of 2008.
In the meantime, the Palestinians must destroy “the terrorist infrastructures” and Israel must cease its colonization work, Bush explained, reminding the two parties of their commitments with regard to the “roadmap” for peace, which they had agreed to in 2003.
The head of the White House emphasized that the negotiations between Israel and the Palestinian authorities under Abbas ultimately seek a peace treaty, which should clarify all outstanding issues without exception, and including central issues.
This designation encompasses the three serious problems around which the disputes between Israelis and Palestinians mainly revolve: the future borders of Palestine, the status of Jerusalem, and the fate of Arab refugees who were expelled from their homeland in the course of the conflict of 1948, which lies in the present state of Israel.
According to the American president, Abbas took the floor and insisted that the coming negotiations should be conducted “comprehensively and thoroughly” and provide a settlement for all points, enumerating Jerusalem, the refugees, borders, colonies, water and security.
When Olmert, for his part, took the floor, he pledged that the forthcoming ceasefire negotiations be conducted “directly and without interruption” and that he would make every effort that they can be terminated before the end of 2008.
Bush has set the deadline for the implementation of his “vision of two states,” a Palestinian and an Israeli one that coexist, at the end of 2008.
Saudi Arabia has disclosed that it has received an assurance from Bush in this sense before it decided to attend the conference.
Arabia, whose participation in Annapolis is a great success for Washington, has launched a plan adopted by all Arab countries that offers peace to Israel, in exchange with its withdrawal from all the Arab territories that had been occupied during the war in 1967, including East Jerusalem.
Annapolis represents a crossroads in the history of the Middle East, especially because it attacks the roots of the conflict and its central points of view,” says the Saudian prince in his interview with the Saudi-funded Arabic-language daily.
According to the prosecutor's opinion, the officers are not responsible
Exactly what happened on Sunday?
As always with this kind of local news, official versions and rumors face each other.
What are the circumstances of the accident?
According to the initial results of investigations by the National Disciplinary Authority (IGPN), a police van drove towards Villiers-le-Bel on Sunday, shortly after 5 p.m.
"He had no deployment, but was on patrol, driving at 40-50 km/h, without a blue light," explains a spokesman for the police.
A green motorcycle of the Kawasaki brade, “without registration, i.e. not released for public road” (see below), arrived, controlled by two young people without a helmet, “at a very high speed”.
"It rammed the left side of the police van after disregarding its advance from the right," a policeman said.
The alcohol control carried out in the vehicle passengers much negative.
The condition of the carriage (dented fender, splintered windshield, torn bumper) testifies to the fierceness of the impact.
Very quickly, already yesterday morning, Marie-Thérèse de Givry announced that she had entrusted the IGPN an investigation into "facts of involuntary personal murder and the lack of assistance to persons in danger," in coordination with Val-d`Oise's main public security division.
In the afternoon, on the occasion of a press conference, the prosecutor stated that the IGPN's determination at this stage excludes the accountability of the police officers.
According to the magistrate, three witnesses of the accident, who were listened to Sunday evening "the version of the policemen, namely that this small motorcycle approached relatively quickly to their left side, have confirmed.
Yesterday, a kind of grooves, certainly caused by the friction of the footrests on the ground, showed that the two vehicles had been hooked into each other until the officers managed to brake.
The vehicles have been seized and will be the subject of a technical assessment.
The corpses of the two victims were obstructed yesterday.
What happened after that?
Yesterday in Villiers-le-Bel, a rumor quickly made the rounds, according to which the police had left the scene of the accident very quickly, and without providing help to the two youths.
Although the IGPN remains very cautious, it would also preclude "any serious error".
According to a police source, “this is a difficult aspect of the investigation, for which further investigations are necessary and in which one has to proceed very carefully”.
Libération met an eyewitness of those few moments shortly after the accident.
From one of his windows he had a direct view of the scene.
"We immediately went out with my cousin, who's a nurse in the hospital, to try to revive her again, that witness says.
There was nothing to do.”
Then he sawed “a completely disturbed policeman who limped.
Two others tried to revive one of the youths, the fourth on the phone.
In view of the tension that had quickly formed around the event and, it seems, due to the certainty that the persons present had about the unexpected situation of the youngsters, the policemen were taken in the car by colleagues who had arrived at the scene of the accident.
Still, according to this witness statement, the rescue workers arrived “about twenty minutes after departure of the police.”
They have also applied heart massages, but in vain.
“I won’t let it be claimed that the police forces did not provide any assistance to the youths.
These young people have been given first aid.
The firefighting firefight arrived immediately.
Everything was done to try to save them,” the prosecutor said.
Tension is rising in Pakistan
On Monday, the Pakistani police knocked down the first demonstrations since the state of emergency had been proclaimed two days earlier by General Musharraf.
According to witnesses, in the city of Lahore in the east of the country, about 2,000 lawyers who demonstrated against the measures taken as part of the state of emergency had been violated by the tear gas grenades used by the law enforcement.
These then threw stones with which they injured the policemen.
At least 350 lawyers were temporarily arrested.
Similar scenarios existed in Karachi and Rawalpindi, where police officers attacked and injured lawyers.
Several would have been temporarily arrested.
In Islamabad, lawyers of the Supreme Court's legal profession have called for a general strike and announced demonstrations, as a result of a wave of about 500 arrests by opposition activists, human rights advocates and lawyers during the weekend.
Monday morning, the roads leading to the Supreme Court had been closed to prevent the assembly.
Further demonstrations had taken place in Multan (center) and Peshawar (north-east).
The lawyers' movement on Monday received the support of religious parties united in the powerful Muttahida Majlis-e-Amal alliance, whose president "urged the population to send flowers to the judges who spoke out against the state of emergency, and those apostasy who have taken an oath from Musharraf.
Musharraf's promise no longer applies
Pakistan's Supreme Court is directly affected by the special measure, which was announced Saturday night.
In the following days, he had to take a position on President Musharraf's re-election in a direct ballot on October 6.
The president of the Court, Iftikhar Mohammed Chaudhry, was removed from office and replaced.
"All that we represent is illegal, anti-constitutional, and contrary to the decisions of the Supreme Court," the magistrate told The News.
Pervez Musharraf's promise to surrender the command of the army and become a civilian president "no longer applies" since the invocation of a state of emergency, the deputy minister for information Tariq Azeem declared on Monday.
He was expected to relinquish his military uniform by November 15, dating when his first term as president ends.
The Vice Minister of Information has also confirmed that January's elections could be postponed.
In addition, the Pakistani government denied the stubborn rumours in the country that President Musharraf would have been assigned a residence by his deputy commander.
More than one-third of pregnancies are still not planned
This calculation takes into account the unwanted pregnancies (24%), as well as the poorly planned or unforeseen birth.
Even today, 40 years after adoption of the Neuwirth law, which allowed contraception in France, one in three pregnancies (36%) is not planned, according to a study by the National Institute for Demographic Studies (Ined).
This calculation takes into account the unwanted pregnancies (24%), as well as the poorly planned or unforeseen birth.
The Ined estimates that the various methods of contraception, such as the pill or spiral, still have a 5-6% failure rate.
According to the institute, which finds that the percentage of pill users between 20 and 40 years of age who use the pill has risen from 5% in 1970 to 60% today, the liberalization of contraception has nevertheless enabled better “control of fertility.”
Nowadays, eight out of ten births are carefully planned.
In one of five couples even calculated exactly to the month, and, if possible, in spring, the season in which it seems most pleasant to have a child.
Spectacular repair at the space station
Astronaut Scott Parazynski and his comrades from the International Space Station (ISS) crew will have taken more than seven hours in space to carry out the spectacular repair of a faulty solar collector that is indispensable for the continuation of scientific work on the station.
A daring phase-out that has achieved several records due to its duration, the removal of habitable modules, the speed at which it was organized, and the “tinkered” tools to carry out the repair.
The problem arose on Wednesday, when astronauts unloaded the Harmony module from space shuttle Discovery, a large cylinder-shaped part designed to soon accommodate European and Japanese scientific laboratories.
This module is a kind of landing bridge for the module laboratories.
But you have to be able to supply them with electricity
Thus, the second task of the crew was to implement a 17.5-ton module for generating electricity via solar panels, which was already located on the ISS, in order to bring it to its final position.
After moving it, the crew embarked on the restructuring of the module's solar panels, whose wingspan is 76 meters.
And in doing so, it happened that one of the collectors, which can be unfolded like accordions, was damaged by a pinched cable over a length of about one meter
The restructuring was halted in order not to damage the collector even more.
The astronauts, who were initially supposed to leave the space shuttle Discovery for a control gear and then to repair another solar collector whose rotation system was faulty, were forced to dedicate themselves to the damaged collector.
And Scott Parazynski was chosen to walk around at the end of the 30-metre-long remote-controlled arm of the space shuttle that had been attached at the station to carry out the repair.
He's one of NASA's best spacewalkers.
Large, resistant, very skilful, he has a well-developed torso and long arms.
The 46-year-old-year-old physician was thus chosen to “operate” the “injured” solar collector.
After assessing the damage closely, he severed the faulty cable and attached a kind of rails to the damaged spot.
He was assisted by a second "Spacewalker" who remained near the lock, and, from inside, by the other astronauts, among them Peggy Whitson, commander of the crew.
The tool he used, a mini hockey racket, wrapped with an insulator to prevent an electric shock on the solar panels that generate several hundred volts, the astronauts aboard the station had conceived and “tinkered”.
When the action was over, Scott Parazynski waited until the collector was fully unfolded before countingly counting his tools and returning to the station.
Yesterday afternoon, the astronauts enjoyed a half-day of relaxation, before seven astronauts returned to the space shuttle Discovery today to start the return flight to Earth on Wednesday.
EAD's title on the stock market sanctioned
After thanking that it will have to spend between €1.2 and €1.4 billion in order to counter the A400M’s arrears, EADS is currently showing a significant descension on the Paris Stock Exchange.
EADS is currently losing 4% to €21,96 on the Paris Stock Exchange.
The group was forced to re-examine its business forecasts for 2007, referring to the arrears caused by the slowly progressing development of the A400M's engine, delayinging the aircraft's maiden flight.
The cost announced in October to compensate for the delay in the delivery of its military transport aircraft amounts to “between €1.2 and €1.4 billion, of which more than a billion for Airbus,” the European manufacturer’s EADS said.
As a result of these costs, EADS will be forced to announce new operating performance targets (EBIT) for 2007 at the presentation of its quarter-year results next Thursday, it added without further details.
The group also states that the assessment of the new costs is related to the six-month backlog.
As a reminder, EADS' business performance fell from 86% in 2006, largely due to the costs associated with the delection of another program, that of the very large A380 carrier aircraft.
Originally, the group had planned an initial delivery of the A400M to the French army for October 2009.
Choosing the status quo
On Sunday, it will not be exciting when counting the polls: the mass of Russians will opt for President Putin's party, United Russia.
After a very closed-off election campaign, it confirms its quest for stability, even authoritarianism.
Three days before the parliamentary elections, Russian President Vladimir Putin sends a message broadcast on the public broadcasters to voters, which has already been recorded, but whose content is being kept secret.
He does so in his capacity as the top candidate of the United Russia, the pro-Kremlin party, for which an overwhelming victory is announced.
Yesterday, the president didn't see through anything when he received the foreign ambassadors, and was content to deliver a new point of attack against the Western powers accused of interferencing - his favorite issue during the election campaign.
"We know the price of a true democracy and hope for honest elections, extremely transparent and open, elections without scams," he added.
From the opposition's point of view, they've misunderstood there.
She denounces an electional campaign stained by oppression.
The communist leader Guennadi Ziouganov says there have never been such dirty elections, not even in the time of Eltsin.
After the suppression of the demonstrations last weekend, ex-chess world champion Garry Kasparovder has been sentenced to five days in prison for unauthorised demonstration, according to his law, according to his lawyer.
“The Russians have preserved the mentality of Homo Sovieticus.”
After these parliamentary elections without tension, two much-furthing questions arise:
Who will succeed Putin as president?
The term of office yesterday announced the date of the first ballot of the presidential election, March 2, 2008; the candidates have until 23 December to make themselves known.
Since he was elected in 2000 and re-elected in 2004, Vladimir Putin cannot run for a third mandate in a row.
And now there is no successor.
What role will Putin play after March?
Prime Minister?
Chairman of the parliamentary majority and the almighty party United Russia, who will select government and president?
Whatever it will be, in the parliamentary or presidential elections, the previous power will triumph.
It's tradition.
"The Russians have retained the mentality of Homo Sovieticus, they are not masters of their destiny," says sociologist Lev Goudkov, director of the Levada Independent Center.
The historian Nikolai Kopossov of the State University of St. Petersburg goes back even further: in this country, which was Christianized for five centuries after Europe, bourgeois society has developed very slowly; the famous community spirit has delayed the development of individuality.
After the liberal reforms of the 1990s, Putin reconnected to the model of a paternalistic state, which was completely accepted by the population, all the more so since this new paternalism was not followed by a certain economic prosperity, says Maria Lipman of the Carnegie Foundation.
Today, the Russians “of their own free will give up to the personal responsibility” which they brieflied after the disintegration of the USSR.
"To think for a long time?"
This phenomenon affects all layers of society, including the business world.
And the administrative elite knows how to maintain the status quo: 65 governors (out of 85 Russian region chairmen) top the UN's local electoral lists.
The new freedoms coming from the West are often synonymous with chaos, as in the 1990s.
The general public prefers stability.
With that in mind, the Russians won't vote in favor of the extremist parties either.
Tamara Tcherednitchenko, a 54-year-old professor, said: “To think for a long time?
Those who are in power aren’t so bad as to detach them?
A difficult memory
After being kept confidential for more than 60 years, Bad Arolsen's Nazi archives, documenting the fates of 17.5 million victims of the Hitler regime, are now freely accessible.
The 47 million documents of the archive, which are housed over a total length of 27 km in Bad Arolsen, could not be viewed until now, except with a “humanitarian” intention, i.e. individually and name-related, i.e. on the query of a former victim or his relatives.
In May 2006, the eleven countries charged with their administration, including Germany, France, Israel and the USA, agreed to make it accessible to researchers and historians.
After the ratification process of this decision has been completed, the opening has been valuable since yesterday.
Delicate information
Historians’ access to Bad Arolsen will make it possible to conduct investigations “in particular about the deportations of prisoners, the inmates of the concentration camps and the state of health of forced labourers,” explains Reto Meistern, head of these archives administered by the International Committee of the Red Cross (CICR).
Until recently, several states, including Germany, were opposed to making these name-related documents available to the public because of the delicate nature of certain information.
For example, the alleged homosexuality or the intellectual disability of some deportation victims.
The occupant lists of the concentration camps or the arrest warrants issued by Gestapo had been compiled after the war for a practical reason: to find civilians who had been deported or subjected to forced labour, and possibly to provide them with evidence of their arrest in order to pave the way for their compensation.
Even today, more than 300 permanent employees process tens of thousands of personal enquiries in Bad Arolsen.
Nevertheless, a part of the scientific community shows only a limited interest in access to Bad Arolsen.
"You can't expect anything fundamentally new in these documents," Pr. Wolfgang Benz, head of the Research Center for Anti-Semitism at the Technical University of Berlin, explained in 2006.
The EU and India want to obtain a free trade agreement in 2008.
Mutual trade between India and Europe is currently reaching €60 billion per year.
The EU is India's most important trading partner and accounts for 18% its foreign trade.
European Trade Minister Peter Mandelson and his Indian counterpart, Kamal Nath, indicated that the European Union and India hope to sign a free trade agreement in 2008.
The EU's foreign ministers had given the European Commission the greeen light last April to open negotiations, with a view to signing free trade agreements (ALE) with India, South Korea and the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (Asean). Mutual trade between India and Europe is already reaching about 60 billion euros a year.
The EU is India's first trading partner and accounts for 18% of its foreign trade.
The EU is also its first foreign investor, but according to figures from the European Commission, India is only the EU's ninth trading partner.
"A free trade pact will advance the two economies enormously and "India would improve its access to a market of 450 million wealthy Europeans," Peter Mandelson confirmed.
Europe forms the first or second market for the ten most widely exporteded products, the European Commissioner further explained. The Asian and European giants annually exchange "goods worth 47 billion euros" and services worth 10 billion euros, this week Daniéle Smadja, chairwoman of the secondment of the European Commission in New Del Delhi, and regrets that mutual trade has not yet reached the European Prime Minister.
(Source AFP)
The Bank of England takes note of the alarm and offers £10 billion for five weeks
The Bank of England expressed concern on Thursday about curbing the currently recurring concern over loan terms, announcing an offer of £10 billion with a base rate of 5.75% for five weeks, an offer with an unusual maturity.
“In order to mitigate the fears that conditions on the financial market will be particularly tough at the end of the year and to give banks greater certainty about the way in which they are supposed to organize their liquidity positions at this time, the Bank intends to offer a significant portion of its savings deposits during the maintenance phase, which begins on December 6, in the form of an open-market operation for five weeks.
The bank further declares that in this way on December 6 at 12.15 GMT 10 billion pounds (14 billion euros) will be circulated, at the property tax rate and only until 10 January.
This is seemingly the first time the BoE has circulated liquidity for five weeks in an open-market operation.
This usually happens for a week.
The BoE adds in its communication that that it "agrees to take further measures to maintain the "interest rates" different from day to date of interbank loans with its basic interest rate.
The day-to-day different interbench interest rate was 5.829% on Wednesday.
The three-month interest rate has leveleled to 6.6%, slightly higher since September 18.
Since the beginning of the financial crisis of the summer, the sums circulated by the Bank of England have been coupled with penalty fees, which had prevented the banks from availing them.
According to certain rumours, intensified by the development of the pound rate as opposed to the euro, many preferred to sign for the sums circulated by the European Central Bank
UMP and NC refuse to review PS's proposals on purchasing power
On Thursday, UMP and Nouveau Centre groups refused to discuss the articles of the socialist bill on purchasing power, which amounts to a rejection of the text.
The text created in the context of a socialist “niche” (meeting on the initiative of a parliamentary group) stated about ten measures that had been adopted on Wednesday by the National Committee of the PS, the bulk of 50% to the employment allowance, the reintroduction of the TIP flottante, a transport cheque or the limitation of rents.
After UMP and NC had voted against examining the articles (180 votes against 115), the session was dissolved after the main discussion, which gave rise to sometimes violent exchanges of words between the majority and opposition, a few hours before Nicolas Sarkozy's televised contribution on purchasing power.
The leader of PS MEPs Jean Marc Ayrault announced that his group will present the entirety of its proposals as an amendment to the bill for the rectification of financies that had been examined last week by the Assamblee.
A new festival is born
As a result of numerous rumours regarding the creation of a new music festival in Philadelphia through C3 presents, the promoter/producer announces the Vineland Music Festival in New Jersey, in collaboration with Festival Republic by British producer Melvin Benn.
“It’s an honor for our company to work with a person who, no more and no less, served as a role model for the current festival movement in America,” Charlie Jones of C3 said.
C3 hosts the Lollapalooza festival in Chicago and the Austin City Limits Music Festival.
Vineland, New Jersey, is a summer destination for camping holidays.
The festival is expected to take place from August 8-10 on a 500-acre private farm about 40 minutes from Philadelphia.
The director of Festival Republic Melvin Benn, formerly known as Mean Fiddler, is one of the producers of the world's most prestigious festivals.
In 1989, he took over the management of the Reading Festival in the United Kingdom, which attracts around 80,000 people every year.
In 1999, Melvin Benn added the Leeds Festival to his portfolio, and then, in 2006, Festival Republic created the Latitude Festival at Henham Park in Suffolk.
Since 2002, Melvin Benn, in his capacity as co-owner, has also been responsible for production, licensing concessions and organisational tasks of the Glastonbury Festival, which can now accommodate up to 177,500 people.
Charlie Jones of C3, describing Glastonbury as the “most inspiring event” he’s ever been at, says his company and Melvin Benn’s company are working alongside each other in parallel to provide the area with a European-style festival event.
“We’ve chosen Philadelphia as a market and are working eagerly with the Fairmount Park Commission to create a festival in the city center of Philadelphia.
We held negotiations with the city and the commission was motivated and enthusiastic about this project.
Regrettablely, we're stuck in political debates regarding the current administration, Charlie Jones continues.
“Luckily we were able to discuss with Melvin Benn and we’re making preparations to create a great festival in an extraordinary place that will ultimately benefit the population.”
C3 Presents revived the Lollapalooza festival in 2005 in the Grand Park Chicago, after making itself in the world of festival-profile together with the Austin City Limits Music Festival in 2002.
The two events in 2007 reached £9.8 million and €11.8 million in gross tax revenues.
These two festivals take place in an urban environment.
"The European model of Melvin Benn is a rural festival, whereas ours rather take place on the urban green spaces," says Charlie Jones.
“The aim is, in one way or another, to merge these two concepts together in order at the same time to give the impression of a rural festival in the European and an urban one in the American way.”
Vineland is located about halfway between Philadelphia and Atlantic City.
"Vineland's community, including the mayor's, takes on the burden of this event with joy," says Charlie Jones.
The latter refuses to disclose the terms of the agreement, but he confirms "that "they are sufficiently interesting to invest in such an event and record a festival that hopefully will have a long life."
Charlie Jones says the festival's recording capacity is "very large," roughly the 80,000 people at the Bonnaroo Music Festival in Tennessee.
He says the invitations have been sent out to the groups, but that nothing could yet be confirmed when they were printed.
"Vineland is going to offer tasteful rock, but the music will be very diverse, hoping to attract all kinds of viewers," says Charlie Jones.
His partners from C3, which has received the top independent promoter prize of the last Bollboard Touring Award, are Charlie Walker and Charles Attal.
Montreal meets the Terminator.
It’s not easy to get the main platform for Hollywood movies to Montreal if none other than the Terminator oppose it.
These lure attempts are among the delegate tasks.
A politically "delicate mandate" because the governor of California, Arnold Schwarzenegger, denounces the "Runaway Productions."
Is it difficult to negotiate with the "governator"?
“You have to be careful, because it’s the industry that decides where it goes.
If she finds that the conditions are favorable in Montreal, she will go there.
Our role is to offer those you think are the best," he says.
The run to the festivals.
For Denis Turcotte, however, the advancement of the cinema there is a top priority at the moment.
The hunt for the Oscars has begun.
At all festivals on the West Coast, although that of Sundance, that of Denver, Seattle or San Francisco, it will be about introducing the films from Quebec, assessing them and making sure the producers give interviews.
The dark age had a successful performance at the 30th Mill Valley Film Festival a few weeks ago.
It will be unveiled at the Palm Springs International Festival in January.
Shake hands with the Devil is on the poster in Santa Monica and will be unveiled at the 30th Starz Denver Film Festival in November.
This month, Quebec has also featured four feature and four short films at the American Film Institute Fest.
Is the stock market facing a final-year rally? Disagreement among experts due to U.S. mortgage crisis
With almost five weeks until the end of the year on the stock exchange, investors are already rubbing their hands with regard to the coming final year rally.
Nevertheless, the experts are unable to make reliable forecasts in this regard.
Banif believes that “purely statistically, the month of December on the stock market is usually accompanied by a positive market development
Nevertheless, it is not advisable to produce forecasts in the short term and only on the basis of statistics. Especially when serious tensions determine the current market
Although other experts do not approve of such a hefty price increase in the last few weeks of the year, they nevertheless note that "the stock market price is determined by the given liquidity of the market and the market participants, which, due to the lack of secure equity funds, mainly invest in bond funds, which is severely affected by the up-and-trending trend towards profitable assets.
As a result, according to these experts, the stock market will rather have a rather lateral price trend influenced by market fluctuations, “particularly because we cannot yet estimate the extent of the possible fall in the interest rate of the US Federal Reserve, an uncertainty that is mainly due to the increased price of crude oil and the risk of the rise in the inflation rate.
Javier Barrio, chairman of BPI's sales division in Spain, agrees that it is a daring time to announcing an imminent market rally.
"The chances aren't very good," the expert assures.
Spain's Ibex stock index has already lost a score of 4% since its peak on November 8 of 15,945.7 points.
However, not all experts share the same negative opinion.
According to Juan Solana, an analyst at Intermoney, the Ibex 35 could successfully close the year with almost 16,000 points as a result of the restructuring of the investment funds and the traditional subsidy made around this year.
Nevertheless, the expert emphasizes that this year things could develop somewhat differently; mainly due to the end-year rally already taking place in the second week of December, and therefore immediately after the bridge day of the Constitution.
According to the analyst, price fluctuations would not affect market tendencies.
According to Federico Olucha, analyst at Bank Inversis, previous assessments of the market situation are at a near-technical level, which could lead to the fact that “a deviation from the index values, the extent of which depended on the consequences of the U.S. mortgage market crisis.
The analyst trusts that these are positive.
Analysts agree on the selection of values designed to drive the Ibex to new highs.
Solana of Intermoney asserts that “this year it’s the turn of the great values.”
Among the value winners, the expert would include the two big banks and some real estate companies, “because the electricity sector will be too expensive at the appropriate time.”
Meanwhile, Federico Olucha of Bank Inversis assures that the rises in connection with the banking and industrial sectors will go hand in hand.
If the rally actually happens, the real estate companies will benefit from it.
“The ACS has been catching up in recent months and we hope that other real estate companies such as Ferrovial and FCC will take the same course.
With regard to the increased consumer behaviour during the Christmas days, periodic consumption will determine market events in the last half of the year.
On the other hand, the expert believes that the technologies will also achieve positive sales results, even if they are represented only in small numbers on the Spanish market.
Five times in seven years
In the last seven years, December showed only in two cases of falling price history.
When the technology bubble burst at the end of 2003, this led the stock market out of its crisis and at the same time initiated the last real final-year rally so far.
This year, when the Ibex 35 caught up by 28.17 percentage points, December showed a 6.68% price increase.
Whether 2008 will be the fifth profitable year in a row (the Ibex has increased by 8.36% since January) and can also boast a final-year rally, will become apparent.
Popular will have its 200 credit institutions open 12 hours a day from Monday to Friday.
Popular has decided to modernise a large part of its stores.
A total of 200 of these are located in Spain's strongest economic regions.
For the institute, which operates under the brand name Popular Max, these branches represent a major milestone.
But also for the remaining Spanish institutes, as Popular as the first banking groupes the jump to extend the opening hours of its branches on weekdays until 20.30h and start on Saturdays from 10h to 20.30h.
The banking group BBVA, which has also repeatedly announced the extension of its opening hours in the branches, has not yet made a definitive decision.
The bank's ongoing negotiations with the trade unions appear to be aimed at an agreement in which 75% of all branches will be closed on Saturdays and open on a weekday in the afternoon, although not for customers.
Whether other banks join the new initiative that adapts to business opening hours will depend on Popular Bank's experienciences.
And this is precisely the intention that Popular pursues with their project.
The aim is to extend the opening hours of those stores that, due to their location, can capture the buoyancy of independent, small and medium-sized companies, but also those of large department stores.
The bank wants to open the new 200 branches over the course of the next 18 months, as these are not new, but changed opening hours.
For this purpose, the bank will redesign the premises of those branches that are already in business zones and at the same time align them with the individual customer profile of the different zones.
Thus, the expansion of the opening hours will be combined with a new interior design of the stores, which is characterized by a more up-to-date, more colorful design.
The responsible bank representative Ángel Rivera assures that the restructuring does not require any major investments, but would be accompanied by an increased recruitment, since a branch no longer needed six, but eight or nine employees.
The branches, which will even be equipped with additional premises for SMEs, will not sell financial products.
A positive experience for the group
Popular has already tested its concept at nine stores in Madrid.
And the experience was positive, according to Ángel Rivero.
The settlements of the Bravo Murillo, O ́Donell and Gran Vía streets have been chosen, which seems to have been a wise decision.
Popular currently manages approximately 2500 branches.
And another 60 who operate under Mundocredit, who specialize in immigrants and also have extended opening hours, even on Sundays and public holidays.
BBVA's model will not be used in these PopularMax branches, as most of them will offer a wide range of non-financial services for sale instead.
"We don't want to compete for our customers," explains Rivero.
With Telefonica, they have tentatively come to the agreement to sell mobile phones using external banking personnel.
For the first time, Spanish private broadcaster Telecinco doubles its market value, overtaking its rival Antena 3.
We made it 
At this rate, the managing director of Telecinco, Paolo Vasile, announced his satisfaction at the close of the stock exchange. The broadcaster led by him had increased its value by two times its rival Antena 3.
One aspect, however, dampened his joy: “That’s exactly what’s hurt me; not that we’ve had higher growth,” he noted.
He emphasized that despite the weak market, they didn’t sink too low.
Telecinco ended the day yesterday with an increase of 2.54%.
This gain reduces this year's losses to 15.66%.
According to Bloomberg, goodwill now reaches the €4,488.88 million, with Mediaset controlling the main share of capital (52%), and Vocento is only 13% involved.
Rival Antena 3 lost 1.52 percentage points yesterday and has registered a loss of 40.5% overall since January, making its market at 2,193.46 million in half.
On the other hand, the market value of other publicly traded communications companies rises to 3,571 million for Sogecable, 2,518.53 million for Prisa (editor of Cinco Día's magazine) to 2,518.53 million and Vocento to 1,779 million.
The comparison with Antena 3 can be deduced from the fact that these are “two similar companies”, according to Vasile.
Uncertainous is the question to what extent we can actually act in terms of stock exchange technology, at what price and how we can make it lucrative at the customer level.
Telecinco announced a week agove its intention to become the first television station this year with more than 1000 million gross profits from advertising revenue. This was assured by Guiseppe Tringali, the managing director of Telecinco and Publiespaña, the advertising company of the group.
If the project were to be implemented, the channel would increase its advertising revenue by 3.6% compared to the 965.4 million from the previous year.
In the case of Antena 3, net sales increased by 1.6% in the first nine stock market months (up to 734.4 million), while net income from the television business could only show an increase of 0.7% (630.4 million).
Analysts see the main factor for Telecinco’s better course of the stock exchange in comparison with the high payout ratio, which the company was able to show up to the present date: In 2006, the company led by Alejandro Echevarría initiated a 100 percent dividend distribution.
Vasile assures, however, the ratio could still vary after the purchase of 33% of Endemol's shares, with Mediaset as a parent company, even if it is still too early for an official announcement.
“We are obligated to the official compromise to pay out at least 50% of the shares, even if we have always indicated much more.
I hope that we can satisfy our shareholders this year, whose share values we have already been able to increase by buying 33% of Endemol shares.
According to the analysts, the market currently has certain loss tendencies.
Lehmann Brothers only recently reduced its profess calculation at Telecinco by 4% this year and by 7% for 2009.
This is the result of a reduction in the anticipated growth rate, which will decline from 10.5% to 8.4% in 2008 and from 5.5% to 5.1% in 2009.
This has also reduced Antena 3's anticipated profit calculation by 4.5% for 2008 and by 7.5% for 2009.
3 options.
1.
Ibersecurities: relies on its high effective return and sustainable potential and therefore recommends the purchase of both shares.
2.
Caja Madrid: measures telecinco (buying) a more advantageous position than Antena 3 as a result of “multiple incentives” and the prosperous market.
3.
JPMorgan: In view of “similar threats” (saturated market and poorer economic climate), recommends resetting expectations in both cases
Congress approves MIFID's directive, which came into force on November 1.
In parallel to the parliamentary drafting, which ended its meeting today with the congress decision in the Senate on the additional/amendaments belonging to the project, the government published the provisions resulting from the new law, with the intention of enabling the entry into force of both regulations by the end of the year.
The representatives of the various companies unanimously praised the efforts of congressionals and Senate MPs, who, in their negotiations and votes, aimed to establish the best possible regulation that ensures the safety of investors and regulates the individual entrepreneurial activities in order to guarantee a generally efficient market.
MIFID, which re-establishes the Investment Services Regulations, pursues an improvement in investor protection in addition to the modernisation of the financial markets, the increase of the claim of control of the Spanish Securities Commission (CNMV) and the promotion of cooperation between the various supervisory authorities of the European Union.
The new directive makes it possible to offer financial advice to both specialized companies and natural or legal persons and also contributes to the establishment of new financial services companies (ESIS), the consultancies that merge with the investment and intermediary companies of securities.
In addition, it expands the number of systems for the execution of financial transactions, so that the regulated circulation markets are added the multilateral systems of the securities transaction and the systems for the internationalization of trading orders, which would eventually compete with the current economic operators.
The law obliges the investment companies to announce the priorities of their company profile, the confrontation and forms of dealing with risks on the market and to register all projects carried out in order to make it easier for the CNMV their control task.
In retrospect, the Economic Commission of the Congregational Committee foreseed further changes, whereby the decision that the debt bond, which is now subject to a “complex” financial resources due to its high degree of risk to the investor, has been confirmed by the U.S. mortgage and banking crisis (U.S. mortgage crisis)
In addition, the entry into force of the directive was used for the legal limitation of the fields of diplomated economists; 30 years after in 1977 the ordinance, which restricted the working areas reserved for economists was adopted.
For its part, the Senate new positions in the financial sector of how that of the economic mediator, who works on a foreign account, does not require authorization from the CNMV and offer its services for the commercialization of products and customer acquisition to several investment companies at the same time.
The harmonised consumer price index (CPI) is up five-tenths in November and now stands at 4.1%.
The indicator used by Eurostat to assess the rate of inflation within the European Union provides for the average price development in Spain in November.
Its publication conforms to the principles of Eurostat and the European Central Bank (ECB), which aim to provide a spatial and quality comparison with U.S. products through the disclosure of the data.
The forecast for the indicator can be prepared prematurely by applying the same procedure as with the harmonised consumer price index, but at the same time using various other statistical modeling methods in order to estimate the information that is not available at the time of calculation.
However, the Spanish Bureau of Statistics (INE) warned that the prematurely determined indicator only served as an indicative value and therefore did not have to coincide with the definitive result, which will be announced next 14 December.
The consumer price index (CPI) will remain at a relatively high 
According to the deputy head of government and Economy and Finance Minister Pedro Solbes, the increase in the CPI in November was a “temporary development”; at the same time, it assured that the price index would remain at a “relatively high” level in the coming months.
Nevertheless, Solbes stated in the halls of the congress building that “even if the present number is not good”, Spain’s difference of inflation to the other members of the monetary union in recent years has remained “more or less”.
In addition, he explained that there were “fewer surprises” in November than in the previous month, in which the government did not expect such a high increase.
"We expected a figure of around 4%," the Minister for Economic Affairs and Finance announced with regard to the forecast published today.
Virgin expects Northern Rock revenue of over €42 billion over the coming three years
Northern Rock's inventories are down by more than half after the bank asked the UK's central bank for an emergency loan due to a crisis in mid-September of last year, and they are now just £12,000 (equivalent to €16,800 million).
At that point, the bank's assets amounted to a record-breaking £25,000 million, a sum that has dropped considerably within two-and-a-half months due to customers' loss of confidence and also despite the government's one-to-the-own guarantee that the money will continue to plummet until the end of the crisis.
The company Virgin, which would continue to run the group after the takeover under the brand name Virgin Money, will have to bring in about a quarter of the income of the British banks, which will be composed there from the investments of the next three years, to acquire exactly the £18 billion still needed to achieve its 30 billion target.
Multimillionaire Richard Branson's group, known through its eye-catching advertising campaigns in the UK, intends to pour £50,000 million (equivalent to about €70 million) annually into promotional and marketing campaigns to achieve its goal.
Northern Rock this week named the Virgin Group, in which three other companies are still involved, as its preferred bidder to run it out of bankruptcy.
Totana's mayor arrested on alleged suspicion of participating in urban corruption
The Spanish police this morning arrested the current mayor of the village of Totano (Partido Popular), which belongs to the municipality of Murcia, due to the urgent suspicion of involvement in urban corruption, which was uncovered this week.
In addition, one of the secretaries of the municipal council and the head of the municipal police were arrested.
José Martínez Andreo represented Juan Morales last May, currently a member of the Partido Popular and at that time chairman of the Totana municipal council, and, according to information from the radio station Cadena SER, could be the original mastermind of the plot.
Officers continue to sniff the business records of the municipality and the Ministry of Urban Planning and do not rule out further arrests in the coming hours.
In addition, Juan Morales' lawyer has been accused, the prime suspect in the case discovered earlier this week of urban planning corruption.
According to the Cadena SER, the lawyer of “popular”, and consequently privileged Deputy Juan Morales was arrested while leaving his client’s place of residence on the basis of important incriminating documents, which should be related to the plot.
In addition, other sources of information report on Morale's supposed order payments to a Galician construction company, which provided for the conclusion of an urban development contract in which the development of a 200-hectare area was decided.
Eight people are currently in pre-trial detention.
Bono agrees to accept PSOE's candidacy in Toledo and, if an electoral victory, in Congress.
Former Defense Minister José Bono has accepted the offer of the government president and secretary-general of the Socialists, José Luis Rodriguez Zapatero. Accordingly, he will lead the list of PSOE candidates in the upcoming general elections in Toledo and, in the event of an election victory by the Socialists, take over the office of Congress president.
This is how José Blanco, organizational secretary of the PSOE, and Bono announced in the press conference after a meeting with Zapatero took place today at the party's headquarters.
According to the head of government, the offer to the former minister is justified, since it is “important in politics to be able to count on the best” and Bono “is among the best.”
In addition, in the event of a victory for the Socialists in the coming elections, the politician from Mancha "apparently seems" to run for the party as chairman of the Congress.
Bono would replace Manuel Martín as congress president, who announced last November 15 that he would no longer compete in the next general elections, to end his career in public politics and instead to advocate for climate protection in the university sector.
The former minister had to admit that since his resignation two years ago, the prime minister had continuously pushed for his return to political events.
Furthermore, he pointed out that, as with the appointment of the vice president for the economy, the party did not want to deprive the citizens who would take over the post of Congress in the event of an election victory and described this as an “act of transparency”.
If he should actually take over the office, he hopes to convert the chamber into a “house of respect and not of insult or shouting” in order to make it appear more and more similar to citizens.
“Regrettably, from time to time you get the impression that the only place where you can’t speak calmly and calmly in Madrid is the Congress; so the president of the government in the Senate has once been interrupted up to 26 times.”
At the same time, he praised the current President Manuel Martín and “his good work” and hinted that he did not want to give the appearance of having already taken over, because otherwise his political opponents would call this “arrogance”.
Climatic propaganda
Since the climate debate relentlessly criticises both the skeptics of climate change and those who place a social and political debate in this regard, the movement as a whole must show a strong cohesion.
Especially if it is a leader of the movement who, as in the case of President Zapatero, considers the fight against climate change to be more important than the fight against Islamic terrorism and even proposes to conclude a “new treaty with the planet.”
However, this proposal puts him in need of explanation with regard to the action or non-action of his government, which has made a decisive contribution to Spain being at the top of the countries that have least met the requirements of the Kyoto Protocol within the EU.
Who are those responsible for not complying with the requirements to which sectors this is attributable, and why did his government, which had proposed the “contract with the planet”, not stopped this development.
In addition, he is now forced to explain to the Spanish citizens the measures he intends against climate change without further ado.
Which companies will be affected by this, which behavior of the citizens should be changed, which individual and collective austerity measures must be carried out and what economic consequences result.
More specifically, who has to sacrifice himself and pay for the rescue of the planet.
However, there have been and will be no such explanations in the future.
Precisely because it's about sacrifice and payment.
Because the measures that can be used against climate change in real terms are unpopular.
They are expensive, inconvenient and threatening for the accounts of companies and citizens.
Even Zapatero’s proposed petroline cent by the PSOE seemed so dangerous in terms of electoration that he was forced to take back the proposal.
Correspondingly, it is now necessary to save the planet with nothing, i.e. with pure propaganda and empty sayings without any real consequences.
With impressive statements, commissions, committees, protocols and new offices.
With his treaties with the planet, the politician, who is otherwise so suitable for election campaigns, does not have to talk or accuse, nor demand, but instead pursue the demanding edition, decorate the meetings and inspire others with his meekness and goodness.
However, this can only happen if the contract does not deviate from its purely virtual nature and therefore does not pretend that the bicycle will become the only means of transport in the future or we are only allowed to refresh ourselves with one compartment.
The mayor and Totana's police chief due to a supposed involvement in urban corruption.
Guardia Civilian has arrested the mayor of Totana, the "popular" politician José Martínez Andreo. In addition, the local police chief, the secretary of the mayor's office and a hotel contractor were taken into custody because of the alleged suspicion of urban development violations.
The city council was arrested at his residence, from where he was taken to the municipal office and on whose premises it is located until now, surrounded by numerous agents, who had also arrived at the municipal administration and with the municipal construction management "Suelo Industrial y Vivienda".
The three arrests are in connection with the manhunts that have already taken place in Galicia, Murcia and Madrid last Monday, in which it turned out that more than 2 million hectares of untapped building ground for urban development projects under the direction of the Galician construction company “Nuaria” were to be redesigned, which was to build 4000 apartments on the property, after the local administration was to return it during the last legislative period.
In addition, two other persons were not directly arrested, but subjected to an investigation, which is initiated and carried out by the first tribunal of Totona.
The first six detainees were presented to the judge today.
Investigating circles are also being investigated against the former mayor of Totana and current independent PP lawmaker Juan Morales.
Furthermore, his ex-wife, owner of a real estate company registered in Totana, as well as his current girlfriend residing in Murcia, are under suspicion.
The chairman and owner of the group “Nuaria”, the lawyer and entrepreneur Manuel N.A. from Vigo, was arrested in the village of Nigrán (Pontevedra), as well as the managing director of “Suelo de Inmonuar” Pablo José M.G., whose company acts as a sub-group of “Nuaria” and is said to have received the bribe.
The investigation is carried out by the municipal authority against urbancies, under the supervision of the central unit of the Guardia Civil, whose representatives carried out eleven further searches in addition to the arrests, most of them in companies that were directly related to the arrests in Madrid, Vigo, Murcia, Pontevedra, Las Rozas (Madrid) and Totana.
At that time, only a single person was arrested in Totana: the owner of a regionally known poultry breeding industry, who is said to have acted as a middle man.
The “Nuaria” assembly was commissioned to build the largest residential complex in the village belonging to Murcia, which should include more than 5000 apartments, a golf course and two spa hotels.
In Totana, the company acquired more than 2.2 million square kilometres of space to be redesigned. The aim was the realization of a construction project that would raise the population of the municipality to 25,000 inhabitants.
The Nuaria group includes other companies such as Autesa and Inomonuar, which promote the sale of real estate; Conyase, which in Vigo operates the parking lots of the Coruña streets, Venezuela and the Plaza Fernando el Católico; Hogartel, whose activity is limited to the sector of direct marketing and Preconiza, specializing in the promotion, sale and installation of components.
