My family doctor presents me with one of one of his long, strange research articles titled “Money leads to the activation of certain areas of the brain, especially if the others get more of it.”
So the feelings arising from the monthly salary payment are dependent on the salary of colleagues.
The study was prepared using a tomographic-magnetic reference analysis at the University of Bonn, and the results were published in the last issue of the science journal Science.
According to Bernd Webers, author of the study and director of the Bonn Brain Research Center Life&Brain, "the activation was strongest when the participant gave correct answers and his teammate failed.
The researchers were able to recognize that the brain activities were always very moderate when both participants were correct with their answer and therefore also got the same content; but if one of them received double, a stronger brain activity also occurred.
I'm not quite sure if it actually takes so much scientific effort to arrive at such a result.
Prosecutor from the Balkans
Carla del Ponte has been relentlessly searching for the criminals of the Balkan wars for eight years. Her seat of office is located in the prosecutor's office of the former Yugoslavia, based in La Haya.
Their mandate will expire at the end of the year and Carla will leave office with a crying and a laughing eye, as the main culprits of the Srebrenica Massacre - in which eight thousand Bosnian Muslims died - namely ex-General Ratko Mladic and former Serbo-Bosnian leader Radovan Karadzic remain on the loose.
Their names are on a list by the Swiss-born director Marcel Schüpbach as a template for the title of his documentary "La Liste de Carla" (2006, not yet launched in Spain); a film that, with the tempo of a political thriller, pursues the Swiss prosecutor from Belgrade to New York in her intention to track down the escaping criminals and to pressure the respective governments.
Which insurance is best for me?
The vehicle insurance, which covers a possible claim caused by the driver, includes a high expense, the amount of which in turn depends on various factors, including age, gender, time of creation of the driver's license, marital status, the type of car to be insured and its use, etc.
Owninging a car is only the beginning.
Because it is even more difficult to find the right vehicle insurance than the right vehicle model.
First of all, it must be clear what we are dealing with here.
Car insurance is an agreement between an insurance company and a policyholder.
This agreement shall be recorded in writing by means of an insurance contract which stipulates that the company will receive the agreed insurance premium and, in return, is obligated to submit the corresponding severage amount in the event of a claim.
The insurance premium is the price of an insurance consisting by several factors, including age, gender, time of preparation of the driver’s license, marital status, type of vehicle to be insured or use thereof.
The insurance companies set their prices according to the statistics, so that in the event of a higher risk of accidents, higher contribution payments are also due.
For this reason, both over 25-year-olds and women need to make fewer contributions, because statistics show that these groups have a lower accident rate thanks to their precaution.
The same also applies to married persons, since, according to the statistical values and therefore, according to the conviction of the companies, these tend to fewer accidents such as single persons.
The benefit attached to the vehicle impairs the frequency of driving and thus the likelihood of an accident.
The more frequently the use, the more likely the damage.
When setting the premium, the insurance companies have to take into account a whole range of points.
For this reason, the driver must also thoroughly study the situation in order to precisely select the car insurance that most closely meets his personal needs.
At this point, we list the most important points that one should consider when choosing an insurancy in order to be able to make not only a performance but also a price-oriented choice.
The under 25-year-olds encounter numerous obstacles in the choice of low-cost insurance, such as the low driving experience and the “young” driver’s license.
Even if it would be best to take out fully comprehensive insurance for ideal insurance coverage, there are cheaper alternatives.
The cautious drivers can choose a full-deducted comprehensive, so that the benefits of fully comprehensive insurance are covered but offered at a low price.
This type of contract implies that the customer has to pay a portion of the amount caused by the damage himself.
“The amount will be determined prior to the conclusion of the contract and on the basis of the insurance premium, which is the lower the amount to be paid,” explains Luis Noguera, Spain’s main representative of First Europe, an online insurance brokerage company.
Another option is extended insurance, which insures the customer against external accident damage, but not against the damage caused to your own car.
In addition, this type of insurance would cover theft, fire and vehicle loss.
Here's some advice.
If you’re under 25 years of age, it’s in your hand to reduce insurance costs; so your credit increases by trying to drive carefully and avoid accidents.
A credition is a reduction in the basic tariff at the relevant insurance company.
On the contrary, a penalty payment includes an increase of said tariff.
The amount credits and penalty payments depend on the company and its income.
If the company has had a high-yielding year, it can allow itself to increase the bonus payments,” Noguera explains.
As soon as the 25-year mark is exceeded, it is easier for drivers to conclude a contract due to the fact that with increasing driving experience, the accident rate decreases.
Thus, it makes the most sense to opt for comprehensive insurance with a deductible.
“Normally, the young people cannot benefit from their previous experience, both in the event of an accident and at the time of driving,” explains the Spanish head of the European company, adding that the majority of accidents occurred in the second year of practice after the “beginner’s fear” has been abandoned.
Nevertheless, it is more advisable for a married customer who does not have children to take out comprehensive insurance; of course, only if the financial situation of the corresponding person also allows.
On the part of the insurers, married couples with children are considered the customers with the lowest accident rate, and their contribution payments are correspondingly low.
In this case, comprehensive insurance with excess (with an amount of around 300 euros) would be the best option. If you have received a high credit, an extended insurance is recommended, which is only used for the third vehicle year.
They drive more relaxed overall and have a greater purchasing power, which allows them to make a higher contribution payment,” notes Luis Noguera.
For pensioners, it is advisable to take out a fully comprehensive insurance with deductible (with an amount around 150 euros), or an extended insurance, which only starts with a sufficiently high credit.
Ultimately, one has to note that car insurance is something very important, not only because it protects the driver against possible accidental damage, but also because it entails high costs that require a thorough selection to be made that corresponds to our personal situation.
Celtic Glasgow hopes to score points for quarter-finals in Milan
FC Milan and Celtic Glasgow will fight for the point they are lackinging to achieve their respective goals even before the conclusion of the Champions League preliminary round. The Italians will need the point to get to the top of their group, while at the Scots will decide on their participation.
The Italian squad already has five points and have already confirmed their participation in the fifth round with a draw in Portugal, while Celtic Glasgow with nine points still needs another one to make sure FC Shakhtar with six points does not catch up with Befica Lisbon with four points.
The Group D's two matches will take place a week before the end of the sixth round of matches, as FC Milan will have to compete at the FIFA Club World Cup in Japan, where it will compete for the first time in the semi-finals, which will take place on the 16th.
So this group will be finished before everyone else and the draw at the Guiseppe Meazza stadium to the ever-expected result, even if both teams will try to win. The Italians will not want to speculate at home the Celtic Glasgow knows that they would be group first in the event of a victory and would thus have a better starting position in the eighth final.
Gordon Strachan's squad could even sufficience a defeat in the event of FC Shakhtar to qualify. Nevertheless, he shouldn't rely too much confidence in such circumstances, because the Ukrainians are entitled to take part in nine-point ties; they had lost last week (2-1) in Glasgow (2-1), but were able to win in the second game (2-0).
Accordingly, the Milans will try to spoil the evening, who in turn hope to reach the quarter-finals of the redesigned Champions League for the second time in a row, after they were defeated in the previous season in exactly the same round by a goal from Kaka in the aftermath (1-0) and by a zero to zero draw on the first leg.
While Shakhtar is waiting for Rossoneri victory, the team will have to focus on its own, as reaching the quarter-finals and its membership of UEFA depends on its victory and defeat of Celtic Glasgow.
Benfica Lisbon, despite their lower standings and without the prospect of continuing to participate in the biggest European championship, could gain third place through a victory and thus play at UEFA.
20,000 people are attending the Spice Girls Tour concert in Vancouver.
The Spice Girls kicked off their first tour after the band's break-up in Vancouver, Canada, in front of 20,000 spectators.
'Posh Spice (Victoria Beckham), 'Scary Spice (Melanie Brown), 'Baby Spice' (Emma Bunton), 'Sporty Spice' (Melanie Chisholm) y 'Ginger Spice' (Geri Halliwell) declared shortly before the start of their concert at the GM Place de Vancouver stadium that it was "excellent".
Around 20,000 people were able to witness the concert in the sold-out stadium, where a video on the history of the band, which had caused a furore at the end of the nineties, was shown.
The reunification and world tour overlap with the release of a new album, which includes their biggest hits and two other new songs, “headlines” and “voodoo”, and which will be released in Spain under EMI’s label.
The five members of the Spice Girls wore golden armbands on stage, which had previously been given by the husband of Posh Spice, English footballer David Beckham.
The sportsman was among the fan crowds in the audience that made the GM Palace glow, even though the nerves of his spouse Victoria had banned him in the previous days.
During the preparations for the Schow, Victoria Beckham announced that the British stard has “not seen our cloakrofe once, and that’s why I’m excited to see what he’s going to think of the performance.”
Sporty Spice, whose departure from the group in 1998 "due to internal disagreements" two years later entailed the dissolution of the group, expressed its nervousness two hours before the concert at a press meeting.
I was shocked.
I was nervous, but I saw that the attitude of the world's public had changed, that the feelings about the Spice Girls had changed.
And that’s why I thought, why not; so I’m glad to have made that decision,” Chisholm commented on the break-up of the band that prompted them nine years ago.
The singer also announced that “the disputes have been eliminated and now everyone is very happy again.”
Nevertheless, many things have changed since the Spice Girls’ last appearance together in 1998.
First of all, according to the report of the television channel CTV, the group arrived in a special aircraft of the Virgin Airlines in Vancouver.
Upon their arrival at the airport, the band was received by an army of nannies.
The five together have seven children.
After the concert in Vancouver, the band will next perform in California, San José (on December 4th) and Los Angeles (Decembeer 5th and 7).
In Madrid, they will take the stage on December 23.
New fears over natural gas dispute between Russia and Ukraine
A new price dispute smoulders between Ukraine and Russia, increasing the risk that Russian exports of fuel to Western Europe will be mitigated.
Most of Russia's natural gas exports to the European Union (EU) are routed through Ukraine and any dispute between the two nations is being watched very closely.
Kiev has warned that if Moscow raises the price it has to pay for the natural gas, it will charge higher transit fees from Russia.
An earlier conflict between the two states last year reduced shipments to EU states.
The recent dispute between Kiev and Moscow is happening at a time when the Ukrainian government and Russia's state gas monopoly Gazprom are trying to reach an agreement on how much Ukraine will have to pay for Russian natural gas in 2008.
According to Gazprom, Ukraine must be charged more, as the group has agreed to pay up to 50% extra for natural gas from neighbouring Turkmenistan.
Analysts say this could mean that Ukraine will have to pay 40% more for Gazprom's imports.
"It is important for us to use our arguments as one of one of the most important gas transit states during talks, Ukraine's Economy Minister Anatoly Kinakh told Reuters.
“Changing natural gas prices should undoubtedly be linked to our arguments regarding the level of transit charges [to Western Europe] via Ukraine.”
Gazprom cut supplies to Ukraine in the last major natural gas dispute between Moscow and Kiev in January 2006.
This subsequently led to a fall in exports to Western Europe, driving up prices drastically.
Russian gas accounts for a quarter of the European Union's supply.
Russia has the world's largest gas deposits, but much of it remains underdeveloped.
As a result, Gazprom is supplementing its own supplies with gas from Turkmenistan and other Central Asian former Soviet nations.
NHS 'must control foreign workers'
Scotland's state health service needs to improve checks on criminal records for workers from outside Europe, a member of the supervisory body said. outside
The recommendation of chief financial controller Robert Black came after a foreign doctor in Scotland was brought into connection with the attack at Glasgow airport in June.
Black also raised concerns that Scotland's state health service did not have precise ideas regarding the number of its foreign staff.
But his report pointed out that other important checkpoints were carried out correctly on a sample of five health authorities.
The main financial controller examined personnel recruited outside the countries of the European Economic Area as a result of the attack on Glasgow airport.
Bilal Abdullah, 28, an Iraqi doctor at Royal Alexandra Hospital in Paisley, was arrested at the airport after an off-road vehicle was driven through the doors of the main airport terminal.
He was one of three doctors who appeared at the Old Bailey in a video transmission in October, and was charged in connection with the suspected car bombing in the UK.
It is expected that the State Health Service will carry out checks in advance of employing foreign employees to ensure that they possess the necessary qualifications, experience and authorisations to operate in the United Kingdom.
Although health authorities use Disclosure Scotland to review criminal records, the agency does not have jurisdiction to obtain this information outside the UK.
The main examiner's report now states, among other recommendations, that the board should apprehensive guidelines of the criminal record excerpt to help employers with checks in different countries.
"Although we have found references to these checks in some personnel records, these have not been routinely carried out," the report noted.
Black said Scottish health authorities would follow their investigative guidelines with a high degree prior to employment but added: "Our report highlights areas in which the system can be improved to ensure that accurate information about the number of foreign health care workers is available."
BMA Scotland chairman Dr Peter Terry said it was "extremely unlikely" that investigations in the run-up to hiring potential NHS staff would identify people who could commit future terrorist attacks.
"The report states that NHS Scotland's current preliminary tests are working well and it is not clear what the added value the recommendations will provide," he said.
Scottish health authorities say they currently have 1,161 foreign employees, the vast majority of them doctors and nurses.
However, the financial controller warned that the number is likely to be higher, also because the boards had problems identifying those employees who are indefinitely on leave.
The investigation further found that established control procedures by NHS authorities in Ayrshire, Arran, Grampian, Greater Glasgow and Clyde, Lanarkshire, and Lothian had been overwhelmingly followed.
This includes passport, work permits, and qualification checks.
The Scottish Government is currently reviewing its policy and guidelines for checks prior to the start of employment for foreign personnel in the NHS.
Health secretary Nicola Sturgeon said: "It was my concern to carry out this work in light of the terrorist attack on Glasgow airport."
She added that the Scottish Government, in conjunction with the health authorities, would consider the recommendations of the report and ensure that best practices in updated guidelines regarding check-ups in the run-up to employment by all employees - including those from abroad - whose publication is planned next month.
Joy Division dominates film award ceremony
Control, the film biography about the late Joy Division singer Ian Curtis has received five awards at the British Independent Film Festival, among others for best film.
Prizes were also awarded to lead actor Sam Riley, who worked in a warehouse before he was casted for the role of Curtis, the director Anton Corbijn, and supporting actor Toby Kebbell.
Dame Judi Dench has been named best leading actress for the Thriller Notes on a Scandal.
And Bond actor Daniel Craig won the Variety Award for putting the British film into the world.
The prizes were handed over in a ceremony at London's Roundhouse.
Contection succeeds Curtia's ascension to fame with Joy Division until his suicide in 1980 at the age of 23.
The black-and-and-white film - on whose soundtrack The Killers, David Bowie and New Order can be found - was filmed for just £3 million.
Nevertheless, he has earned the Dutch photographer Corbijn, best known so far for the iconic cover of the U2 album Joshua Tree, two prizes - as best director and the best directorial debut.
Rock music was the focal point of another award winner at the ceremony - when Julian Temple's film about Clash singer Joe Strummer received the award for best documentary.
The award for the best performer went to Viggo Mortensen for his role as the driver of a Russian mafia boss in David Cronenberg's London-playing thriller Easter Promises.
Ray Winstone, who has a reputation for playing tough guys, has picked up the Richard Harris prize for his outstanding contribution to the British film.
The 50-year-old, whose films include Scum, Nil by Mouth and Sexy Beast, is currently filming the Indiana Jones sequel The Kingdom of the Chrystal Skull.
Winstone said he was proud to get the award, which was named in honor of the Camelot and Harry Potter star Harris, who died in 2002.
"It's been people like Richard Harris and Albert Finney who opened doors to people like me," he said.
“I’ve been fortunate enough to meet Richard Harris a few times.
He's my kind of person, a great actor of his time, I'm truly honored.'
The independent film awards, known as Bifas, are selected by a group that includes filmmakers, actors and festival organizers.
Bifa supporters and sponsors include Mike Figis, Ken Loach, Ewan McGregor, Samantha Morton, Bill Nighy, Meera Syal and Michael Winterbottom.
208 arrested in Saudi Arabia for a terrorist plot
Saudi Arabia uncovered yesterday that it arrested 208 militants for belonging to cells who had planned an imminent attack on an oil facility as well as clerics and security forces.
The world's largest petroleum exporter's state-owned television said one of the cells had planned smuggling missiles.
Al-Qaeda sympathizers have launched a campaign against the monarchy, which has been an ally of the United States since 2003 as a result of the invasion of Iraq.
A cell of eight militants, led by a foreign national, was plotting an attack on an oil facility in the eastern province, according to the television report.
Saudi Arabia has formed a 35,000-strong rapid intervention force to protect facilities following a failed Al Qaeda attack on the world's largest petroleum processing plant in Abqaiq in 2006.
The report, with reference to an interior ministerial statement, says 18 of those arrested belonged to a cell led by a "missile expert" who immigrated illegally into the country.
He reports that the militants had planned to smuggle eight projectiles into the kingdom.
According to the report, another 22 of the detainees were planning to kill clergy and security forces.
The government has warned clerics to do more to stop Saudis seeking Iraq to join forces with Al Qaeda militias who want to fight the Allied forces and the U.S.-backed Shiite Muslim government, considered heretical by the hardliners among the Sunni Saudis.
Al-Qaeda militias regard many clerics in Saudi Arabia as in cooperation with the authorities in supporting the policies of the royal family that dominates the government.
The militias - accompanied by appeals from Osama bin Laden, the Saudi-born Al Qaeda leader to aim at the pro-Western Saudi government - are targeting foreign residential areas, government buildings and facilities of the energy sector.
"This was a very great effort by the security forces over the last... five months," an Interior Ministry spokesman told state television.
The arrests also affected a "media cell" of 16 from Medina, which aimed to promote "Takfiri thinking" - the ideology of radical Sunni Muslims who support violence against Muslims who are branded as unbelievers and apostates.
Among those arrested were 32 people accused of providing financial support to the militias.
The climate on Venus could reveal an outlook on Earth's future
Venus may have once been a pleasant place to live, scientists said yesterday.
Data from a European spacecraft on an orbit show that the planet resembles a remote twin of the Earth even more than was previously conscious.
Venus is Earth's closest planetary neighbor.
The two worlds have about the same size and have similar planetary orbits, but Venus is slightly closer to the sun.
Billions of years ago there would have been conditions on Venus that would have been very similar to those on Earth and could even have been habitable, scientists believe today.
But because of the unbraked carbon dioxide greenhouse effect, the erosion of the atmosphere caused by solar radiation and a slower speed of rotation, Venus embarked on a very difficent development course.
Today, the planet is deprived of water and has a surface temperature hot enough to melt lead and is devastated by hurricane-strong winds and lightning storms.
The findings of the European Space Agency's Venus Express spacecraft, which has been studying the planet for a year, provide a sobering look at how the Earth could develop in the face of catastrophic climate changes.
Professor Fred Taylor of the Department of Physics at the University of Oxford, one of the heads of the Venus Express Mission, said: "It becomes clear why the climate on Venus is so different to that of Earth, while the planets are otherwise quite similar."
“Our new data makes it possible to construct a scenario in which Venus began billions of years ago like Earth, possibly with a habitable environment.”
'Enriched uranium' seized by police
A kilo of radioactive material, reportedly enriched uranium, was seized by the Slovak and Hungarian police yesterday.
A police spokesman said that three people have been arrested and that the material will be examined.
He did not confirm whether it was enriched uranium, but said it could have been sold for $1 million (£480,000).
All still open
Mitt Romey said he didn't consider it worthy of a presidential nominee to be questioned by a snowman.
Even so, the Republican nominee for the presidency ultimately agreed to face a debate on YouTube in which ordinary voters record and submit questions on video to send them to the candidate.
None of the questions raised on Wednesday night, November 28, were as ridiculous as the snowman who questioned Democrats about global warming in the other party's YouTube debate.
But the questions, and the answers, this time accurately reflected the state of the Republican Party breaking into next year's elections.
It's not known, just like CNN's producers, who supported the debate, selected the YouTube clips to be shown to the budding candidate.
However, in the first half-hour of the debate, which was devoted to a single issue, the focus was on immigration.
The issue is one of the hardest issues for Republicans.
The CNN asked a group of undecided voters to serve a dial when they heard things they liked: the television outlet noticed peaks of approval as the candidates spoke strictly about the borders.
The evening's sharpest and longest-serving exchange was between Rudy Giuliani, the national frontrunner, and Romney, who has pushed himself forward in the pivotal polls in Iowa and New Hampshire.
Giuliani tries to shake off the call that he had been lenient about illegal immigration when he was mayor of New York.
He attacked Romney by accusing the former governor of hiring illegal immigrants for work at his home.
The two dealt particularly fiercely as questioners asked whether one of the candidates would support an "amnesty" (the Illegals would give a regular status and the possibility of citizenship).
Insignificant candidates like Duncan Hunter, a California congressman, and Tom Tancredo, a congressman from Colorado, managed to follow up on this issue.
None of the two men have serious chances at the nomination, but both stay in the race, perhaps because they've hawned earlier and harder than others on the immigration timpacks.
At least in the field of immigration, candidates broadly share the same views: tougher borders, intolerance towards illegal migrants.
This issue, along with fears about toxic toys from China or other concerns about the rest of the world, are helping Republicans to forget or minimize the differences between liberals and Christian Conservatives.
But the debate clearly became problematic when other issues were raised.
Mr Giuliani was asked why he had supported arms restrictions in the past.
John McCain and Romney debated whether simulated drowning should be considered torture.
One of the questioners asked if the candidates believe every word of the Bible.
This led to Giuliani taking the view that even though the Bible was his preferred book, he may not literally believe the story of Jonah and the Whale.
Another questioner asked if Jesus would support the death penalty.
Mike Huckabee - now contrary to all expectations in first place in a recent Iowa poll thanks to his long-standing Christian conservativism - said the death penalty was painful but necessary.
When he was urged whether Jesus would endorse them, he laughed about it and said Jesus was too intelligent to apply for political office.
The race remains exceptionally wide open.
Huckabee's rise in Iowa coincides with Ron Paul's recent donation successes and his applause storms in Wednesday's debate.
These two are shot up out of nowhere, because the two wings of the party have not yet been able to agree on one of the better-known and more traditional candidates.
In 1980 and 2000, a candidate has reunited the party from the beginning and convincingly united with a victorious message about America's greatness and a winking to both wings of the party.
This time the party is divided and the result is far from clear.
Pain of the Labour Party
Gordon Brown has discovered yet another pit to fall; his way out of it remains the same.
For British scandals, this one isn't particularly slippery.
No nobility titles appear to have been sold, no politician's Paris hotel bills paid, no extramarital toes have been sucked.
But uncovering that a wealthy businessman has concealed donations to the British ruling Labour Party over more than £650,000 ($1.3 million) over four years under the name of other people, and that the party has failed to report this one is something that the embattled Gordon Brown would have done well.
When two months ago the prime minister evaded the demand for an election, stupidly insultinging the intelligence of Britons by claiming that opinion polls had not influenced this decision, there was hope that he would provide a robust, efficient government.
Instead, as Vince Cable, the increasingly astute representative chairman of the Liberal Democrats, put it, the once-dreaded Brown has evolved from Stalin to Mr. Bean.
Northern Rock's awkward rescue, an ailing bank, showed regulatory weakness and poor judgment under fire.
Poorly thought-out proposals to amend the tax laws have raised business associations against the government.
The tax authority loss of personal data of 25 million people showed unimaginable administrative scuffles.
A trio of retired generals launched an uprising in the upper house in protest against cuts in military spending: being ministers of the armed forces, they claimed was a part-time job.
Now there's many donations from David Abrahams; Peter Watt, Labour's general secretary (and their former fulfillment leader) has already resigned.
None of this means Brown irrevocably sets off towards resignation.
Nevertheless, governments can reach a turning point after which they find it impossible to govern.
Neither like nor do people trust politicians, but they normally withhold their scepticism when a new force takes office.
If it is once clear that it is unlikely that a prime minister will improve the situation, and he doesn't even need to be at it for long, this postponement is over: the public service is beginning to leak; the cabinet ministers are starting to chat; the press is looking for bad news; and the government becomes defensive and aimless.
In one way, Brown's position is particularly difficult because, unlike other political leaders, he can't claim to be a new broom.
As much as he'd like to deny Tony Blair, he'd been his top lieutenant.
The problems with Northern Rock and the tax jurisdiction documents, for example, are related to decisions Brown made as chancellor of the Treasury.
And even if he wasn't directly involved in Blair's misadventure - as in the long "money for nobility titles" investigation of political party funding - voters may assume it was him.
Brown's situation isn't quite as bad as she looks
The misconduct isn't as terrible as the Conservatives describe it.
As with Mr. Bean, no one died, or has been completely swindled.
And the antidote, painful as it may be, is to deliver what Brown's naturally Stalinist tendencies have always promised: a reliable, solid and slightly brutal government.
The new talk about political donations is a good example.
The garboesque Abrahams, who compares his donations to his charitable gifts, he does not want public recognition for his benevolence.
This may be the case, but he didn’t want the decision.
The laws against anonymity for major political contributors are clear and they exist for a clear reason: the public has a right to know who can buy access to those who govern the country.
Watt, who says he would have known about Abraham's agreement, but not that she's illegal, will of course have to face some questions; just like Jon Mendlesohn, Labour's main financial collector, who was told about the strange donations in October.
Yet Brown isn't involved so far: there's nothing to suggest he knew about these confusions, although most of the payments were made after taking office in June.
So far, the Prime Minister has done half of the work.
He quickly condemned his party's violation of laws, but he watered down that determination by gripping for a well-known instrument, an investigative committee on the issue - to make the measure full, from a retired bishop and a judge.
In this case, the "lessons to be learned from it" are not very complicated.
If the law has been broken, it's a matter for the police.
Apart from that, there are other things that Mr Brown has to do, such as finding a permanent chairman for the Independent Committee on Standards in Public Life, which has been unoccupied for six months, and to move forward with a major reform of political party funding.
That would require Labour's hardliners to cooperate with the Conservatives and possibly give up the special financial relationship with the unions - bitter pills for a comrade like Mr Brown.
But he should be aware of some partisan facts.
Conservatives now have clear leadership in the opinion polls; he's had two years to win back voters; and the only way to achieve victory is through unassailablable righteousness and solid competence.
Card sharks
Is the lending crisis affecting America's credit card market?
When blood is in the water, it's only natural for dorsal fins to swirl excitedly.
Now that America's real estate market is ailing, predators are targeting the country's credit card market.
Goldman Sachs analysts recound credit card losses could reach $99 billion if the risk of contagion from underfunded loans spreads to other forms of consumer credit.
Signs of tension are clearly visible.
There are rises both in write-of-the-outs and the default rates, which measure the proportion of loans that aren’t retractable or more than 30 days late.
HSBC announced last month that it has taken a $1.4 billion fee collection from its U.S. consumer financing, partly because of the weakness of credit card holders.
It's too early to panic.
Losses and delays are still low.
According to rating agency Moody, the third-quarter leasure rate of 3.89% was almost a full percentage point below the historical average.
The deterioration in rates can be explained in part by technical factors.
A change in America's private bankruptcy law laws in 2005 led to an abrupt case of bankruptcy filings, which in turn account for a large portion of credit card losses; the number of registrations (and thus default fees) would increase again, whether the general conditions for borrowers deteriorate or not.
The industry also reports solid payment rates showing how much of its debt consumers pay off each month
and confidence in credit card-based securities is quite strong, despite the standstill in other corners of structured financing.
Dennis Moroney of TowerGroup, a research firm, expects spending volumes for 2007 to ultimately be 25% higher than last year.
There are certainly direct channels of infection between the real estate credit crisis and the credit card market: it is more likely that consumers will take out credit card debt, now that homeownership loans are drying up.
However, the card issuers look to the flow of money instead of capital values, which is why falling house prices do not necessarily trigger a change in the creditworthiness of the borrower.
They can even be beneficial for the card issuers.
The incentives for consumers to continue to pay mortgage installment decrease if the real estate is less value than the value of the loan; as a result, card debt will become more important under repayment priorities.
Card issuers can also react much faster and more flexibly than lenders to the stormier conditions by changing interest rates or credit limits.
This should theoretically reduce the risk of a rapid revaluation of assets.
"We won't wake up one day and completely revaluate the loans," says Gary Perlin, Capital One's chief financial adviser.
Even if a sudden underfunding crisis in the credit card market is unlikely, the risks of a sustained downturn are considerably more real.
If lower house prices and a credit mitigation push America into recession, the industry will undoubtedly face a more grim future.
Keep looking out for these shark fins.
British bankers plead guilty in Enron case
Three British bankers who were supposed to be brought to justice for their role in the fraudulent system with Enron's former chief financial officer Andrew Fastow, on Wednesday, mended their petitions into guilty.
David Bermingham, Giles Darby and Gary Mulgrew had originally pleaded innocent for seven cases of telecommunications for allegedly conspiring with Fastow in a secret financial fraud to enrich themselves at the expense of their employer.
The proceedings had been scheduled for January.
But during a hearing in court before U.S. District Judge Ewing Werlein Junior, everyone pleaded guilty for a case of fraud using telecommunications.
The three former executives of Greenwich NatWest, a unit of Royal Bank of Scotland Group PLC, became known in the UK over the course of extradition proceedings that lasted two years.
In the United States, their case is a loose end to an investigation launched after Enron's collapse in 2001.
The accusations originally filed against them in 2002 drew Fastow's attention to the fact that he was the target of an investigation by the government in connection with the Enron decline.
The indictment against the bankers - called the "NatWest Three" - claims they came to Houston in 2000 to concoct a fraudulent plot with Fastow and his former top adviser Michael Kopper.
Greenwich Natwest had invested in a subsidiary of Enron's partnership controlled by Fastow, who was the architect of countless fraudulent Enron machinations that have helped rip the firm into bankruptcy.
In early 2002, the bank had estimated its stake in the subsidiary at zero, but the three British men knew that in reality it had considerable value.
A company under Kopper's control acquired the bank's shares in the subsidiary for $1 million.
The bankers paid Kopper £250,000 for a stake in that company.
According to the indictment, Fastow falsely objected to Enron that the energy company would pay $20 million to Greenwich NatWest for their shares in the subsidiary.
But in fact the $20 million went to British bankers, Fastow and others.
The bankers received $7.3 million, while Fastow, Kopper and others raked in about $12.3 million, according to the indictment.
In January 2004, Fastow pleaded guilty on two charges of conspiracy for his role in the Enron's bankruptcy.
The British trio were arrested three months later.
Fastow is serving a 6-year prison sentence in Louisiana federal prison.
Kopper is serving a sentence of three years and one month at a facility in Texarkana.
Each of the three men were on the loose due to a $1 million bail requiring them to live in the United States in anticipation of legal proceedings.
Enron, once the nation's seventh-largest company, crumbled in bankruptcy in December 2001, after years of accounting tricks could no longer obscure the billion-dollar debt or make faltering ventures seem profitable.
The collapse wiped out thousands of jobs, more than $60 billion of market value and more than $2 billion in pension plans.
Enron founder Kenneth Lay and former chief executive Jeffrey Skilling were convicted last year for their roles related to the Enron collapse.
Skilling is serving a sentence of more than 24 years.
Lay's convictions for conspiracy, fraud and other charges became void after he died last year for heart disease.
Not at the very bottom of Moscow
The price of a Gulfstream G550-branded aircraft, equipped with a Rolls-Royce engine and enough range to fly non-stop from Moscow to Los Angeles, is at least $54 million.
But that's change for some of the rich Russians.
"It accommodates up to 18 passengers," said Dan Firer, a Gulfstream representative.
“But none of you asks for so many seats because it’s for a private family or a corporate president.”
Firer was at the Gulfstream booth last Friday at the millionaire's exhibition, a four-day orgy of flaunted consumption hosted at a nested exhibition centre on the outskirts of the capital.
In addition to Gulfstream, more than 200 other luxury vendors had set up exhibition stalls, hopinging to interested new Russians in everything from mahogany-panelled yachts to diamond-coated car grills that would be appropriate for 50 cents.
Firer carefully targeted a glossy orange Porsche at the next booth.
"That's a nice car, but it's change compared to us," he said.
“After the United States, Russia is the second-largest market for private aircraft.”
Fuelled by an oil boom, the Russian capital seems as flooded with money as Dallas in its heydays.
"We went through the same phase in the 1890s, when some Americans came to gigantic wealth and went to Europe in droves where they were considered materialistic," said Marshall Goldman, a scientist at the Davis Center for Russian and Eurasian studies at Harvard University.
Like the Sau-Arab high-flyers of the 1970s and the Japanese in the 1990s, the Russians emerge as the most visible money issuers in the world.
The most exclusive luxury brands are looking for their share of the gold rush.
Elsewhere in Moscow this week, a conference of the luxury industry Donatella Versace, Tom Ford and Bernard Arnault, the chairman of LVMH, has attracted to exchange ideas on how to sell high-priced goods in markets such as Russia.
(The conference is organized by The International Herald Tribune, owned by the New York Times Company.)
Russia has $53 billionaires who own a total of $282 billion, according to the Forbe's list of the richest people.
In addition, 103,000 Russian millionaires belong to the second class, which together are worth $670 billion, according to a study on the development of private wealth in the former Soviet Union written by economic experts from the Center for Economic and Financial Research, a panel of experts here.
(The World Bank says 15.8 percent of Russians lived below the subsistence level in 2005.)
"There are hundreds of millionaires in Moscow - that's why we're all here," said Nicole Marais, a sales and marketing representative of David Morris, a London jeweller.
She had visited the fair for the first time together with the brand's Russian retailer, the Soho Jewelry Group.
“Women are all well-groomed in an exemplary manner and the men are all worth a fortune.”
As they move through the halls Sunday night in evening clothes, two real estate agents, Evgenia Yumatova, 21, and Svetlana Turovtsova, 26, describe themselves and their clients as millionaires.
"I've looked for the cars and I'm trying to choose between infiniti, the Mercedes and the Porsche," said Ms. Yumatova, who delivered the designer clothes she was wearing, like a red carpet veteran with Joan Rivers.
The fair seemed primarily aimed at the new-riches, the type of brand-conscious Russian buyers who would be willing to stand in traffic jams for several hours to visit a tracious warehouse that reproadinably put Cadillac Escalades, pocket knives made of platinum, Mövenpick Eiskrem, bespoke billiard tables, apartments in Dubai and Gund teddy bears.
The public was able to buy tickets for €30 (44.57 dollars).
"The Russians are interested in learning which brand is the most expensive and best," said Mr Gijrath, who patriotically sat at an exhibition stand of Dutch porcelain manufacturer Royal Delft in front of a wall-sized imitation of a Rembrandt picture of hand-painted, blue mosaic stones.
“Of course, Russians are more interested in Klimper-Klimper.”
In fact, traders here are saying that the Russians with old money (10 to 15 years old according to Russian standards) have already moved from large-gauge logos to the kind of sober brands with limited quantities that wouldn't market themselves at a trading show.
Nevertheless, several thousand people, whether millionaires, envoys of millionaires or simply tourists pushed through the halls every day.
And some of them bought.
On the show's first night last Thursday, Roman Ershov's general manager of King Koil beds here said he had sold two $70,000 mattress sets, crafted from silk, cashmere and cotton with sewn-in gold threads.
Also on the first night, Mikhail Lyubiumov, a sales manager for A1 Moto, a company that equips high-end cars with high-end alarm systems, said his company had sold the glaring orange Porsche for $170,000 to a Russian millionaire.
By Sunday afternoon, Andrei Karapetian, the president of L.A. Connection, a company adapting luxury cars to customer requests, said he would have sold three car cooler grilles for a Rolls-Royce phantom that resembles interlinked chain bracelets for $55,000 each.
He showed a reporter's car tire designed for a Russian casino owner, with spokes he had covered with colored Swarovski crystals, making it look like a roulette wheel.
The cost: $22,000 for a four-purse.
"The Russian millionaire, who wants to have ostrich leather wheels or a picture of his wife's face from cubic zirconium, won't necessarily ride on these wheels," Mr Karapetian said.
"He's going to keep it in his 30- or 40-wagon garage."
Even though many of his Russian clients are wealthy, Mr Karapetian said the phrase "Russian millionaires" wouldn't express much exclusivity at the moment, given the rise in local property prices.
"If you bought an apartment in Moscow three or four years ago, they're presumably already millionaire, at least on paper, if not in cash," he said.
“There’s more of them than you think.”
Those who weren't millionaires could at least pretend they'd be rich at a booth operated by Alliance Continental, a Russian asset management company.
The company had set up four British-style red telephone booths that were crammed with mountains of false million-dollar bills.
Azad Karryev, the company's marketing director, said its employees had printed out 500,000 false banknotes for the millionaires fair.
Passers-by stopped to throw the piles of money, mobile phone photos as they sit back in the midst of spare richness, snapped by friends, or simply to pocket a couple of millions.
"You see, in Moscow, everyone can feel like a millionaire," Mr Karryev said.
Botched operation suspected at death in New Jersey
It was the day before Thanksgiving when Ronald Livingston looked through the large glass window of the Just a Little Healthier restaurant here and watched an ambulance at the white Cape Cod stop two doors further down on West Scott Avenue.
Soon after, there was a police car there.
And then there was another.
"Then I saw the van of the medical morporaiser approaching," Livingston, an oversighter of the restaurant, said on Wednesday.
“That’s when I knew someone had died.”
Authorities learned that the dead wife Olga Arroyo, 41, was an assistant to the director of the Newark Police Department.
Investigators are now trying to determine whether she died because of a procedural carried out by an unapproved cosmetic surgeon whom police did not identify and who worked at the house on West Scott Avenue 212.
Authorities have not yet determined what caused Ms. Arroyo's death, and are hopeful that toxicological investigations, the results of which may not be available for weeks, will help clear up a mysterious death that has sent a grief by Newark Police's leadership.
Ms Arroyo had been an assistant to the police director, Garry F. McCarthy, since September 2006, and her voice was known to many in the department.
"She was loyal, hard-working and stubborn," Mr McCarthy said of Ms Arroyo in a telephone interview on Wednesday.
"She was my pit bull.
She was protecting me.”
McCarthy said Ms Arroyo was the wife of Jimmy Arroyo, a former Newark police officer who works for the alcohol control commission, and that she leaves behind two sons, 14 and 11, and a stepdaughter.
Eileen Walsh, a spokeswoman for Union County prosecutor Theodore J. Romankov, said investigators would try to determine whether Arroyo died as a result of unapproved surgical treatment.
"That's one of the possibilities," Ms. Walsh said.
“It’s an ongoing investigation.”
Authorities said they had arrived at the house at about 3 a.m. in November.
And they found Ms Arroyo on a sofa.
She's been declared dead on the spot.
More than a week has passed and the authorities have communicated little about their investigation or about the owner of the house, which is listed as Flor Margarita Castro.
Walsh didn't want to say if Ms Castro was questioned by authorities.
Investigators are trying to determine if a portion of the house has been used as a medical practice.
On Wednesday, no one opened the door to Ms. Castro's house, where a wilted rose bush stands in the scarce front yard and a Dalmatian barks behind the house.
A blue barrel inscribed with "Ethyl Vanillin" - used as a replacement for vanilla in food and perfumes - sat in the parking lot next to a door leading to the basement.
Two mountain bikes hung from hooks on one side.
McCarthy said that when he first arrived in Newark after spending 25 years with the New York Police Department, Ms Arroyo was invaluable, helping him find his way around the provincial and harsh political culture in his new city.
"She said 'you have to pay attention to this or that,'" he said.
“She was always here when I got here and she was always here when I left.
It was embarrassing.”
Hopes that the central bank will lower interest rates lead to a rise in world stock markets
Shares jumped yesterday on both sides of the Atlantic after market hopes the central bank will try to reassure credit markets through further interest rate cuts were boosted by appeasing comments from the vice chairman of the U.S. central bank.
Stock markets rose in London, New York and across Europe after Donald Kahn's No. 2 number of Ben Bernanke, the central bank's chairman, said the policy needed to respond "flexibly and pragmatically" to the ongoing pressure in financial markets.
Reassuring markets in a clear effort to reassure, given signs of emerging credit tensions, Kohn said recent turmoil had "partially reversed some of the improvements in market functions from the late part of September and October."
Should the increased turbulence persist, it would increase the possibility of further tightening of financial conditions for consumers and businesses, he said.
The comments have been picked up by investors as a signal that the central bank is willing to intervene and stabilise credit conditions with new capital injections into the U.S. markets and possible further interest rate cuts.
The remarks also helped reassure Wall Street, given further bad news from the U.S. economy, now that sales of U.S. real estate reached a new record low in October.
The consequences of the financial turmoil have been underlined by Bear Stearn's announcement that it would eliminate 650 jobs, or 4 percent of its global workers, in a cost-reduction measure to offset hefty losses from its lost bets in the U.S. mortgage lending sector.
Only 20 jobs are expected to disappear in London, where the bank employs 1,500 people.
In New York, the Dow Jones industrial index jumped 331 points, or 2.55 percent, to close at 13,289.40, while the broader-produced S&P 500 index rose 2.65 percent.
In London, the FTSE 100 index rosed 165.5 points, or 2.7 per cent, to close at 6,306.2, adding more than £39bn to the value of UK defaults.
Germany's Dax index rose 2.6 percent and France's CAC40 rose 2.3 percent.
Previously, the resurgence of credit pressure on the markets had been underlined after strong demands were awarded at a considerable 4.7 per cent at an auction of three-month guarantees from the European Central Bank, the highest rate since April 2001.
The pressure on the Bank of England, the U.S. Central Bank and the European Central Bank to follow and to feed more funds into the money markets in London increased when credit tensions saw the three-month "Libor" rates for borrowing between UK banks at 6.59 per cent to their highest since late September, following a continuous increase since November 21.
England faces decision on Owais Shah and Ravi Bopara
Here two dilemmas, each of them almost as difficult as the other.
Who's supposed to beat as number 6 for England in Kandy this weekend, Owais Shah or Ravi Bopara?
And, no less relevant in a broader context than the first test against Sri Lanka, who's supposed to make the decision?
The second question is important because David Graveney, the election chairman, on call at least under this particular title, is here to help head coach Peter Moores and captain Michael Vaughan to decide, and because the table of High Morries, the English managing director, gradually fills with applications for national electoral offices, if not for those part-time.
Whoever doesn't get the sixth racket position will be unhappy, but life is often unfair and the sport is undoubtedly.
It’s never easy to nail down exactly what allows a person to reach the top of their profession, while someone else will never get beyond the lowest rank, sometimes for lack of happiness instead of ability.
It's not just in cricket, where selection is difficult, where judgments are needed that are always subjective at the end.
Is it the right time for Bopara to step up?
Is it fair to Shah if he does?
The same may also be asked into another area, whether, for example, John Edwards or "Tubby" Banerjee, both intelligent young executives with good deals who know the business well, should be promoted.
Who can tell what the right class is for a cricketer, unless he is given the opportunity to swimme or go under.
Of course, figures have a big impact on decisions, but chances have.
Within a team, a cricketer must catch the captain's eye at the right time if he can take a wicket and a racket must get regular chances on order if he wants to impress as a one-day cricketer.
Catching the eyes of the selectors is more complicated.
There's been many who are better than last season than, for example, when James Anderson were, who is apparently going to play in the first Test, even if Stephen Harmison declares himself healthy and how the wind cones into the nets.
Anderson took 32 first-class wickets with 33 runs each to earn a position in this tour.
Chris Tremlett took only 29 with the same modest average, but both had their highlights and hoping for even more.
Whether Shah or Bopara plays at Asgiriya Stadium can only be a matter of view.
Choosing could decide the careers of both men, or it might make little difference.
Four years ago in Sri Lanka, Gareth Batty played in every test and was successful in the first, with five wickets and 40 useful runs, it was Paul Collingwood, at that time still struggling to consolidate, who made the breakthrough into a significant Test cricketer and who, despite scorining a double-hundred against Australia in Australia, may still be underestimated.
It is likely that Graveney, Vaughan and Moores, after another discussion in the wake of today's net session at Asgiriya Stadium, will follow their conservative instincts and gut instinct for Shah's greater experience about Bopara's youthful enthusiasm.
There are arguments for both.
For Shah, his sparkling and valuable 88 Test debut innings in Bombay, 43 top-notch hundreds and only six of Bopara, who is seven years younger at the age of 22, and an excellent record against screwed-on bullets.
But Shah made only ten runs in his two innings in the May against West Indies in the Test at Lord, his homeland arena as a player from Middlesex, and this one, plus his immediate rejection from the pick-up as Vaughan was healthy to take back his position for the next game, seems to have taken the momentum out of his beatting, which was still evident in Bombay.
In contrast, the all-rounder from Essex has shovel-by-shovel joie de vivre.
He isn't impressed by anything on a cricket pitch and his posture away from that was thrown to the point when he recently knocked over a four-wheel-drive vehicle in an exhibition for England's sponsor Volkswagen.
He suffered minor injuries and presumably a shock, but the first thing he did was getting his Vodafone (sponsored, of course) and calling his team-mate Alastair Cook, who had been watching this from a distance, and enthusiastically saying: "Did you see that?"
Bopara is a much better fielder, he cones his middle pace with some effect here on the playing fields, which seems to suit him and beats with confidence and skill.
Choosing the right man is one thing to choose the right time another.
Maybe that's the real art of choice.
I tend to give Bopara his chance now, which doesn't mean Shah might not be doing well if, as Ceylon's tea set suggests, he's the one who's going to play here this weekend.
Buses bring refugees back to work - and to combat Al-Qaeda
The first busloads of Iraqi refugees from Syria arrived in Baghdad yesterday, with a stream of displaced persons whose funds or visas have expired, and others who hoped the city would return to normal.
Some had even come back to fight the rebels who had driven them out of their homes.
At Mansour bus station, even before the arrival of 800 people from Damascus in an official government convoy of a dozen buses, the terminal was busy with refugees from Syria and Jordan who had returned in private transport.
"I decided to go to Syria after killing a lot of doctors in Baghdad," said Sabah Al-Qubaisi, a Sunni doctor.
“I came back today because of what I’ve seen on TV and what I’ve read in the newspaper about what happened in Baghdad, and how it would be safe now.”
They say that the Mahdi army has stopped killing people," he said, referring to the Shiite militias of the militant cleric Moqtada Al-Sadr, who has ordereed to reset his powers at the present time.
Qassim al Atwani, a 38-year-old Shiite teacher from the majority Sunni part of Amariyah, returned with his two children because he could not renew his Syrian visa and because the regime does not allow Iraqis to work.
His Shiite neighbors in Amariyah called him to tell him that a local U.S.-backed militia has helped drive Al-Qaeda out of their bastion in the west of the capital and urged him to return.
One of the new arrivals from Jordan was the leader of a Sunni and Shiite tribe who had been expelled from his home in Sleikh in northern Baghdad by an attack with hand grenades after calling on his fellow citizens to fight Al-Qaeda.
He had come back to fight.
“Two days ago, some of my friends called me and told me that they started one the anti-Al-Qaeda militia in Sleikh and that they needed my support and that of my boys.
That's why I returned today," said Sabaah Alu Obeidi, 62.
Many recounted that they had been lured by the increasing calm in the capital since the U.S. troop increase and the growth of local "groumments affected citizens" who defend their neighborhoods armed and in cooperation with the American military.
But more spoke of having been forced home by visa problems and dropping funds in Damascus, where more than 1.3 million Iraqis had fleded in recent years.
Salaam al-Samarrai, a 51-year-old three-year-old father, expressed general suspicion among exiles that Nouri al-Maliki, the Shiite prime minister, had asked the Syrian government to impose visa restrictions on the refugees to force people to return.
More than two-and-a-half-million people are believed to have fled from the sectarian death squads in the country.
"We were doing well until al-Maliki visited Syria and the government urged Iraqis to stop giving visas to force them to return to Iraq," the Sunni pensioner said.
"I can't trust a government that supports the criminals who burned my house after I walked away."
One part of the reason for the decline in violence is that Sunnis were expelled from Shiite residential areas and vice versa, leading to the creation of many places where tensions have subsided.
There are fears that a sudden influx of refugees will stoke sectarian tensions again and there will be conflicts.
Small Saarland, good education
The smallest federal state is a leader in vocational training and even shines with the international orientation of its universities.
Only in an educational field does the Saarlanders push the same deficit as the rest of the country.
"I explained yesterday for the 50th time that we are not French" - this slogan the smallest federal state self-deprecating for its 50th birthday.
France and Luxembourg play a major role in the Saarland education system, which ranks fifth in the education monitor of the Institute of the German Economy Cologne (IW).
The universities are already cooperating closely with neighbouring countries by tradition.
The university in Saarbrücken was founded in 1948 by the French.
To this day, students can complete their studies here with a double or even triple diploma.
Correspondingly, the proportion of foreign students is 15.6 percent.
But the state children also bring the Saarland in above-average numbers to the universities - many of them without a graduation at high school.
"In Saarland you use the vocational education system," says Axel Plünnecke, education expert at the IW.
More than one in five adolescents acquire their eligibility to study at a vocational school, making Saarland more than seven percentage points above the national average.
The possible combinations of vocational and academic training are also comparatively developed.
Compared to the number of the population, equipping with dual degree programs is the second best in Germany.
However, the number of engineering graduates has fallen by a third in recent years despite practical training and increasing demand.
This is why the Saarland Ministry of Education has now committed the universities to a marketing campaign for engineering subjects.
"We initiated a number of reforms at the general education schools after the first Pisa study," says a spokeswoman for the Ministry of Education.
This is how the government introduced nationwide comparative tests.
In order for the children to start to learn earlier, the last daycare year is free of charge - a model that other federal states are now also copied.
Whether the performance of the pupils improves sustainably will only show future comparative studies.
At Pisa in 2003, the proportion of high-risk pupils shrank from 29 to 20 percent.
Overall, however, Saarland did not increase as much as other federal states.
Bayern are expectinging better.
And to the annoyance of the Saarlands, foreign language skills are not measured at Pisa - in this area one could otherwise score.
Because the language of neighbors is mandatory at grammar schools - even if the Saarlands aren't French.
Chávez converts the clocks
In Venezuela, the clocks are soon ticking differently than in the rest of the world.
On December 9, President Hugo Chávez rolls back the timepieces in the South American country by half an hour.
Meanwhile, in Spain, Chávez becomes a joke figure.
According to a new law, according to a new law, the clocks are set minus four and a half hours at world time.
With this, the South American state gets a time zone that does not exist in any other country in the world.
U.S. hostile Chávez justified his decision, among other things, by saying that the United States had imposed the division into time zones at full hours.
Chávez also announced on Wednesday (local time) because of a hostage affair the break-off of relations with neighboring Colombia.
The left-wing nationalist said that as long as the conservative Alvaro Uribe remains president of Colombia, he would not maintain relations with either the head of state or the Colombian government.
"I can't do this because of my dignity alone," said Chávez, who is pushing ahead with constitutional reform that the citizens of the oiling country will have to vote on Sunday in a referendum.
With the reform, he would significantly expand his power in the country.
Uribe's decision to revoke Chávez's mandate for negotiations with Marxist rebels in Colombia, which was about the release of hostages.
Uribe "spitted in the face" of Venezuela with his decision, Chávez had criticized on Sunday.
Chávez is also on everyone's lips in Spain.
"Por qué no te callas" (why don't you shut up) is the phrase that more than half a million people have already downloaded from the Internet.
Spain’s King Juan Carlos, who was annoyed by the constant interludes of the left-wing populist at the recent Ibero-American summit, spoke to him and had crossed his mouth.
Germans threaten wage minus in 2007
Despite the strong economy, according to a forecast, German employees threaten a real wage loss this year.
Experts cited a whole bundle of reasons for their pessimistic prognosis.
According to a calculation by the Hans-Böckler-Foundation, employees could expect less in the salary account in real terms than in the previous year.
"This is due to modest wage developments, VAT increases and price increases for food and energy," Gustav Horn from the Institute for Macroeconomics and Economic Research told the Bild newspaper.
New burdens could also apply to workers for unemployment insurance.
Finance Minister Peer Steinbrück told the "Neue Osnabrücker Zeitung" that the planned reduction of unemployment insurance to 3.3 percent at the turn of the year was correct and reasonable in the current situation.
However, the Federal Employment Agency should not be embarrassed about having to ask the federal government for loans or grants, the SPD politician was quoted.
On Tuesday, it was revealed that the price explosion in food and petroleum products drove the inflation rate to its highest level in 13 years.
Overall, prices rose 3.0 percent year-on-year.
Venus is Earth's infernious twin
Despite its hostile atmosphere, Venus is more similar to Earth than previously thought: The European spacecraft "Venus Express" has discovered lightning in the atmosphere and wind vortices at the poles, as they also occur on Earth in winter.
For nearly three decades, scientists suspect it's flashing on Venus.
Now the European probe "Venus Express" has yielded the detection - it detected electromagnetic waves at the edge of the atmosphere: The lightning on Venus shoots at an altitude of about 56 kilometers from cloud to cloud, reports Christopher Russel of the University of California in Los Angeles in the science magazine "Nature".
According to the famous "primordial soup theory", lightning has created life on Earth: thunderstorms in the ocean are said to have stimulated reactions that led to the formation of amino acids, the building blocks of life.
“If there’s ever been life there, it must have been in very early history,” explains Sean Solomon of the Carnegie Institution in Washington.
Today there should hardly be anything alive on the surface of Venus: the atmosphere is about a hundred times denser than on Earth, there are sometimes temperatures of 500 degrees.
"Venus may be Earth's devil twin, but it's a twin," Russel said.
Why Venus, which is so similar to Earth in mass and size, has undergone such a completely different development is the fundamental question of the Esa mission.
The launch of "Venus Express" in November 2005 was a milestone for Venus research, as since the beginning of the 1990s, there has been no probed specifically to explore this planet.
The latest research, which is now presented in several "Nature" articles, underscores not only the uniqueness of Venus within the solar system.
"They also show that our neighboring planet's similarities with Earth are greater than initially assumed," the Esa announces.
Thus, in addition to the already well-known whirlwind at the North Pole, the researchers also found a cloud swirl at the South Pole, which rotates much faster.
The phenomenon is reminiscent of aerial currents on the earthly winter hemisphere, writes a team of researchers led by Giuseppe Piccioni from the Italian Istituto di Astrofisica Spaziale e Fisica Cosmica.
The solar system's secondmost planet has almost the same mass as Earth and, with a diameter of 12,100 kilometers, is almost as large.
Also referred to as the morning or evening star, the planet circles around the sun in just under 225 days.
However, it revolves much more slowly around itself: a Venus day lasts 243 earth days.
Although Venus, with surface temperatures of 460 degrees Celsius and a pressure of 92 bar, is hardly reminiscent of Earth, scientists assume that both planets were formed under very similar conditions.
Water accumulated on both, which remained in oceans on Earth, but on Venus was lost into space.
And while carbon dioxide is fixed on Earth in carbonate rocks, it accounts for the bulk of the atmosphere on Venus.
Köhler defends Merkel's meeting with the Dalai Lama
Federal President Köhler calls for an end to the party dispute over the reception of the Dalai Lama in the Chancellery.
Indirectly, he criticized the SPD because of its attacks on the chancellor after the meeting in September.
Federal President Horst Köhler is worried about Germany's reputation and has called on the grand coalition to end the dispute over the Dalai Lama's reception.
"We shouldn't present a divided nation to the outside world on this issue," Köhler told Handelsblatt.
At the same time, Köhler signaled understanding for the reception of the Dalai Lama by German Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) in September.
"Human rights have universal validity for me, even if I have respect for the diversity of cultures," Köhler said.
Everyone knows that Germany must maintain sensitive balances in foreign policy, the Federal President emphasized, but defended Merkel against the accusation that she is running a new China policy.
“The chancellor has not refrained from the traditional one-China policy.
China knows that too," Köhler said.
Foreign Minister Frank-Walter Steinmeier (SPD) had indirectly indicated displeasure about the reception of the Dalai Lama and had therefore been attacked from ranks of the Union.
Steinmeier had complained, among other things, about the Union's "showcase policy" in foreign policy.
After the reception of the Dalai Lama in the Chancellery, there was severe irritation in German-Chinese relations.
Thus, the Chinese leadership has not only unloaded German Finance Minister Peer Steinbrück (SPD), but, according to SPIEGEL, has also frozen the so-called Strategic Dialogue of the Foreign Ministries of both countries.
China's Prime Minister Wen Jiabao has now called on German Chancellor Angela Merkel to admit the Dalai Lama's reception as a mistake.
China rejects it when European politicians officially receive the religious leader of the Tibetans, Wen Jiabao reaffirmed the Chinese position.
The actions and words of the Dalai Lama showed that he is trying to secede Tibet and the other Tibetan region, which also includes other today's provincies, from China.
Schäuble warns of riots like in France
Street battles with the police, gunfire, burning cars: Wolfgang Schäuble warns of riots like in France.
Everything must be done so that social dissatisfaction in Germany does not unload violence on the streets, demanded the Federal Minister of the interior.
Barricades burned, rioters shot at the police with shotguns: After the severe youth riots in France Federal Interior Minister Wolfgang Schäuble (CDU) fears a similar development in Germany.
“We have to make sure that we don’t build up social explosives and discharge into violence.
We have to take this seriously," he told the "Neue Presse" published in Hanover.
Not every young person copes with the rapid change of social reality.
Federal governments, states and local authorities were already working forcefully to reduce problems, Schäuble said.
This does not only concern the integration of migrants.
"From the integration summit to the Islam Conference, this is already showing its first successes."
However, it is too early for all-clear.
Schäuble pointed out at the same time that there are non-urban and sociological problems in German suburbs.
In northern suburbs of Paris, teenagers had fought heavy street battles with the police over the past nights.
Cars and public buildings also burned in Toulouse.
However, the chairman of the Turkish community in Germany, Kenan Kolat, called for major integration efforts by the German government.
Faced with riots in France, Kolat warned of "imitation effects."
The situation in Germany is not as explosive as in France.
However, there is still a need for action.
Kolat pointed out that the unemployment rate is "above-average" among young people under the age of 25.
Above all, it is necessary to provide more training places.
Kolat stressed: "We have to give the young people a perspective."
However, the chairman of the Turkish community added that "social control is taking place in the families" in Germany more than in France.
In addition, many young people with a migrant background did not want to destroy what their parents built here.
Kolat warned: "But we still have to pay attention."
“We must quickly give young people the opportunity to participate in work and training.”
Meanwhile, it remained largely quiet during the night in the Paris suburb of Villiers-le-Bel.
Hundreds of the police were deployed, a helicopter flew over the area with headlights.
Competent authorities reported three arrests as well as a few burning bins and cars.
Since the previous day, an array of around 1000 security forces ensures peace of mind.
The police will crack down as long as it is necessary, Interior Minister Michelle Alliot-Marie announced after a visit to the suburbs.
During the night on Tuesday, around 80 police officers were injured in riots in Villiers-le-Bel.
In the aftermath, the riots spilled over into Toulouse.
The riots ignited from a traffic accident in which two youths had died on Sunday.
According to the first findings of the prosecutor's office, the police bear no blame for the death of the youths.
In the realm by Angela Wolke
The chancellor's far-religious appearance documents that the Coalition is at an end in terms of content - and that nothing more can be expected from it itself.
In which country does this chancellor actually live in?
After her bleak appearance in the general debate of the Bundestag, which was wurthy and content-poor, it must be said: not here - not in the country that would finally have to be governed by it.
Angela Merkel rejected the accusation that she focuses too much on foreign policy with a badly arrogant sentence.
This is provincial, she said, showing that she does not want to understand what the critics are about.
In the Bundestag, it has clearly documented for everyone how much their accusation applies.
There was neither concrete interest nor even a feeling for the problems in the country.
Instead, Angela Wolke presented herself: a detached politician who flees into foreign policy and herself remains there in the rough.
Example of climate protection: It praises itself unchecked for the decisions of Heiligendamm, but does not say how the climate goals should be met in this country.
In general, the self-praise - so much immodesty the CDU leader would have failed two years ago.
Now she clings to the phrase that the upswing arrives at the people.
In doing so, she damages her image as a pragmatist who solves problems rather than the world.
But in Angela Wolke's empire, domestic political problems are a fringe phenomenon.
Child poverty and the concerns of low earners?
She's got a few subordinate sentences left for it.
It is significant how often she expects when it comes to the children's surcharge.
Merkel needs three attempts there to bring out a halfway straight sentence.
It stumbles as if on foreign terrain when it becomes concrete.
Department of Attack
In the Republican presidential candidates' YouTube debate, two favorites Rudy Giuliani and Mitt Romney deliver a tough exchange of blows - and must now fear an outsider candidate.
The tension of the candidates is already discharged on the first topic.
He has made New York a "refuge for illegal immigrants," accuses Mitt Romney's opponent Rudy Giuliani.
"I'd say his own house was a haven for illegals," Giuliani replies, accusing the former governor of Massachusetts of privately employing immigrants.
Candidates blaze to the attack: Because the Republican base still has no clear favorite, the eight presidential contenders use the debate on CNN primarily to discredit their adversaries.
The self-made 30-second videos that Internet users have sent in via YouTube are little more than cue givers.
"With this kind of isolationism, we've let Hitler come to power," accuses John McCain of his opponent Ron Paul, who is the only one in the round calling for an immediate withdrawal from Iraq.
Romney, too, is vigorously reading the Levites when he refuses to condense the so-called "waterboarding," an interrogation method in which drowning is simulated as torture.
However, the important topics of the campaign are now beyond the Iraq war and torture debates, is shown by the reactions of the audience in St. Petersburg in Florida.
Whenever conservative values are praised and future tax increases are rejected, applause burns up in the auditorium.
This shows once again where the weaknesses of the two favorites lie.
"I was mistaken at the time," Romney declares crunched, in front of a grassroots and televisance for the fact that he was still one of the abortion advocates in 1994.
Giuliani, on the other hand, comes down when he is asked about his opinion on the literal interpretation of the Bible.
Musharraf reigns in plainclothes for the first time
A day after his resignation as head of the army, the Pakistani leader Musharraf has been sworn in as president.
This ends eight years of military domination in Pakistan.
President Pervez Musharraf was sworn in for another five-year term on Thursday.
Pakistan's television broadcast the ceremony live in the presidential palace of the capital Islamabad.
Musharraf vowed to respect the constitution and act for the good of the nation.
On Wednesday, he handed over his office as army chief to his confidant Ashfaq Kiyani, bowing to international pressure in particular from the United States.
Eight years after his seizure of power in a bloodless military coup, the former general now governs for the first time as civilian head of state.
With his resignation as commander-in-chief of the armed forces, Musharraf had met a core demand by the opposition and the West.
Another central demand is an end to the state of emergency that Musharraf imposed on November 3.
Soon after his swearing-in, Musharraf, according to Attorney General Malik Muhammad Qayyum, will lift the state of emergency and thus fulfill another demand from the West.
The country's Supreme Court, which Musharraf occupiened with him weighing lawyers following the imposition of the state of emergency in early November, had dismisselled all objections to the disputed re-election of the head of state on October 6.
Meanwhile, five soldiers were killed and four others injured in an attack on a military convoy in the northern tribal region of North Waziristan.
According to an army spokesman, the bomb exploded on a road near the border with Afghanistan.
The fathers of Krautrock return
Germany's super group of the seventies makes music again: The three band members of Harmonia performed their consoles again at a concert in Berlin.
The revival is accompanied by a recording from her great time "Live 1974".
Even then, they were much more famous abroad.
The scheduled sensation then takes place in the form of three set gentlemen at the evening table.
Everyone has their box in front of them, their cables and above all their buttons.
Dieter Moebius sits in the flower-white shirt at the age of 63 in the middle.
Left flanked by Michael Rother, 57 years young and owned by laptop and guitar.
Viewed from the auditorium, Hans-Joachim Roedelius, 73, bends on the console.
Everyone contributes sounds, sometimes even a beat.
And sometimes it accidentally blends into a conventional piece of music.
This doesn't seem to bother the gentlemen themselves.
The fact that the band Harmonia is making music again has caused some excitement among Krautrock lovers.
Targeted requests revealed that although the name buzzed through collective pop memory, it was by no means susceptible that this band had been painfully missing for 32 years.
But why shouldn't the German also be allowed to celebrate his living legends from the 1970s?
Especially, what is misleading Krautrock was always more true in England and America than in their own country.
When the music industry awarded the survivors of the Cologne-based group Can the Echo Prize for life's work in 2003, people looked into questioning German pop faces in the hall.
When the Red Hot Chili Peppers also thanked the bands La Düsseldorf and Neu! in the laudation, the amazement knew no bounds.
At concerts, Michael Rother later squatted like an old buddy at the Red Hot Chili Peppers on stage.
The problem of ignorance at home and transfiguration abroad is one thing.
The memory is overwhelmed with harmonia because the trio only existed for two years and during this time also unfolded a far less power of action than was subsequently assigned by others.
Moebius and Roedelius had already made music with the artist Conrad Schnitzler under the promising name Cluster.
At first they played in the basement under the Berlin Schaubühne.
Afterwards in Düsseldorf, for economic reasons for a record label maintained by the church.
Rother had helped out briefly at Kraftwerk and then gained a reputation as a guitarist at Neu!, perhaps the most radical German group at the time.
Around 1973, however, Rother succeeded the cluster musicians into exile after Forst im Weserbergland.
There, in the Old Weserhof, a 16th-century ruins, they lived as city fugitive communards.
Less ideologically than in Commune I, where Roedelius was still allowed to plague himself as a nanny.
Rather in a pointed artist's existence.
Supposedly, there was also a pennant of the singing association Harmonia Ottenstein in bulky waste and a name for art.
Some things Harmonia held on to it.
The first album was called "Musik von Harmonia" and "De Luxe".
Because as serene and modest as the music also sounded, the band suffered material hardship.
Ricing did not only serve self-discovery.
The now released CD "Live 1974" was recorded at a concert in Griessem, in front of an almost 50-strong record backdrop, as someone thinks to remember.
From Rother at that time came the desire to force harmonia and occasionally practice.
For Roedelius and Moebius an immoral demand.
The band separated in 1975.
Rother landed in the esoteric-spherical and secured his existence with albums such as "Flaming Hearts."
Cluster was resuscitated in the Eighties and has been more successful than Harmonia ever.
Thanks to Herbert Grönemeyer’s live album in Griessemer.
Its record company Greenland has set itself the goal of cultivating Krautrock as its own German contribution to living music.
New! has already been excavated like Roedeliu's solo work.
The physical resurgence of Harmonia takes place in the Berlin House of World Cultures.
Publicly subsidized as part of the Woldtronics Festival.
Julian Cope is there, the Briton, Altpunk and author of the handbook "Krautrock Sampler".
There's the love parade inventor Dr. Motte, too.
This would outline the entire range of the influences of Krautrock on subsequent pop music.
Harmonia completes the vacancy of the supergroup.
Brian Eno's sentence floats with the most peculiar sounds in the hall: "The only true music, you own the future."
There are pictures that show the producer with Harmonia in the farmer's room.
Projections of other black-and-white photos of wired equipment are used to create the legend.
Before that, the three veterans are engaged in improvisation again.
No longer by sticking microphones to the organ and on the drums to elicit unheard-of tones from the instruments via self-made effect devices.
Today, electronic music looks like electronic music.
Three inventors deepened into the technical.
The choir of the house doesn't change that either.
Under the direction of Barbara Morgenstern is coughed and whispered under sound clouds and sung in a nice place with Emphase "Aaaaaaah!".
Krautrock has always been a very English term.
It was never about rock.
The roots were searched for less in blues than in music academies (can), technologies (power plant) or on the run from artistic and social conventions (clusters).
Anyone who shouts "'68!" is not entirely wrong, and Harmonia does not allow themselves to be disturbed even now.
Even the most self-evident is thoroughly reconsidered on stage.
Where does music start and when does it stop?
This can rob your nerves or give fleeting moments of great happiness.
Richard Gere just isn't cynical enough
"The Hunting Party" and "Murderous Peace" deal again with two films with the Balkan war.
But both do not convince.
Either the concepts rely too much on action and cleavers or they are too touchy.
Richard Gere can't help in "The Hunting Party".
The good news at first: Although the war criminals Karadzic and Mladic are still hiding in the mountains of the Balkans, especially there, in the gloomy hinterland of the Bosnian war, the actor Richard Gere has now been found again.
We had lost sight of the most beautiful man of the 1980s, because too many nice-insignificant roles in feel-good films for women and too many press conferences on the subject of Buddhism drove out the desire to look.
Now Richard Gere is suddenly the most important argument to look at "The Hunting Party - When the Hunter Becomes the Hunted" (Why do movie titles always have to be as long and confused today as locomotive driver collective agreements?).
He provides a wonderfully tongue-in-cheek depiction: Basically, his war reporter Simon Hunt is what could have Gere if fate and the film industry were not so gracious: a man who has passed his very best for a long time, but who, even as a ruin-like monument to his decline, has retained considerable remnants of the former charm.
But despite this trump "The Hunting Party," along with the German production "Murderous Peace," which also kicks off this week, continues only the endless series of failed Balkan war movies.
With "Mörderic Peace" you can make short processes: The film is about two German soldiers in Kosovo (Adrian Topol, Max Riemelt), who rescue a Serbian from Kosovan snipers, whereupon one promptly falls in love with them.
Although it is welcome that not the Serbs are portrayed here as the sole root of all evil, the television-flat film loses itself all too quickly in geballer and action.
It remains a kind of "alarm for Cobra 11" without a highway but for it with war.
On the other hand, "The Hunting Party" can be explored by way of exemplify why there has actually never been a really good Balkan war movie.
The story of three U.S. war reporters setting out to track down the Serbian supremainer-war criminal "the fox" might have become a new "Apocalypse Now" with another director and another screenwriter (both in personal union Richard Shepard) at another time.
Because the stories are strikingly similar: Both times small teams go into the woods to accomplish something that the militaries can't do and both times it's going to take a journey into madness.
One of the reasons for "The Hunting Party" isn't that you don't just let Simon Hunt be the run-down cynic he's being introduced to us at the beginning.
No, the script also has to give him a romantic affair with a later murdered Muslim Bosnian.
"The Hunting Party" finally goes down when the reporters within the last five minutes in a time-lapse procedure succeed in what NATO, the CIA and all bounty hunters in the world have failed to do in five years: catch the war criminal and leave it to the Muslim Bosnians for just revenge.
This is so bad that you want to appeal to the International Court of Justice in The Hague for crimes against cinematic logic.
The film has a deeply human concern.
He wants to denounciate that Karadzic and Mladic are still running around freely, and he suspects the Americans and the French (here they are supposed to agree for once) not to do the persecution too intensively due to collusivion due to collusivion.
But the well-intentioned and the evil are sometimes so similar that a famous Brecht poem comes to mind.
It says about the wooden mask of an Asian demon on the poet's work table: "Compassionably I see the swolen forehead veins, suggestive: how exhausting it is to be evil".
Movies like "The Hunting Party" have just those thugs, however, suggest how exhausting it is to be good.
The problem isn't limited to the genre of the Balkan war movie.
It has recently been reported in this newspaper that all of Hollywood's Iraq films were big flops.
You can add that there has never been a good South Africa movie.
Almost always applies: When a star drifts around in the trouble spots or minefields of the world, then something like "Rangoon" with Patricia Arquette, "Beyond all borders" with Angelina Jolie or "Blood Diamonds" with Leonardo DiCaprio.
Possibely all the conflicts are simply too close in time.
After all, decades had to pass before Hollywood could make a good film about the Holocaust with "Schindler's List".
Some film will probably still ripen - the Vietnam works "The Deer Hunter", "Apocalypse Now" or "Full Metal Jacket", which are now considered classics, have not been unanimously celebrated as masterpieces at the time.
But perhaps today we know where every mass murder in real-time comes on two screens at home, also just too much about the world’s conflicts.
And that robs scriptwriters of the carelessness it takes to tell good stories.
After all, "Being or Non-Behaving" or "The Great Dictator" have become so good because Lubitsch and Chaplin did not yet know how bad the Nazi regime really prevailed.
Maybe you can't want to be good if you're trying to make good movies.
Perhaps you have to have the sarcastically distanced artist's gaze of the novelist Lawrence Norfolk.
When he was questioneded into parallels between his historical novel "Lemprière's dictionary" and the Balkan war, he replied: "That's just because some things never change.
Big cities will always be terribly dangerous, people will always be murdered in horrific ways and there will always be war in Yugoslavia.”
Von Beust reaps criticism for Olympic cancellation
The mayor's departure from Hamburg's application for Summer Olympics has met with severe criticism from politics and business.
Many want to keep fighting for the games on the water.
However, von Beust urges realism in assessing the chances.
Hans-Jörg Schmidt-Trenz, the chief executive of the Hamburg Chamber of Commerce, on Wednesday described a waiver as "not good for the business location."
No other project has in recent times developed such great motivating forces in the city as the Olympic idea.
"Without this goal, the sports-policy momentum would not be able to be sustained to the same extent."
Schmidt-Trenz urged the Senate not to bury the project, but to find a new long-term approach to "which assumes a changed timeline."
Mayor Ole von Beust remained with previous statements on Wednesday that Hamburg's Olympic ambitions were unrealistic at least until 2028.
"It's not Hamburg that decides on an Olympic application, but the German Olympic Sports Confederation," von Beust told WELT ONLINE.
He referred to the decision of the Sportbund, expected at the beginning of December, on an application by Munich for the Winter Games 2018.
"For us, this preference means that we will have no realistic chances in the period until 2028 envisaged so far."
Nevertheless, the dream was not dreamed out for all time, said von Beust.
The Hamburger Sportbund (HSB) regretted Beust's statements.
This gives the impression that Hamburg will no longer apply for the Olympic Games in the next 25 years.
"It's too early to obstruct an opportunity by burying the plans already at this point in time," HSB President Günter Ploß said on Wednesday.
In addition, a waiver of the application was contrary to the statement of the head of the Senate on the occasion of a reception to the General Assembly of the General German University Sports Association.
There von Beust described Hamburg's application for the Universiade 2015 "not as a replacement" for the Olympic Games in Hamburg.
The university sports association had only last week nominated Hamburg as an applicant city.
The SPD Citizenship Group accused the Senate of failing to use valuable time for housing construction in Hamburg.
"The development of the Little Grasbrook has been delayed by the Senate for years because he putted Olympia on the map and excluded housing construction," SPD lawmakers Carola Veit and Jan Quast said.
While the SPD has always demanded an integrated planning for Olympia and a later residential use, the CDU has unilaterally relied on the "event factor".
The application for the 2012 Summer Olympics had generated a great deal of enthusiasm in Hamburg at the time.
Hamburg, however, had failed at Leipzig.
They'ren't Warhol's boxes
Anyone who associates the terms Swedes and wooden boxes thinks of Ikea, but not Andy Warhol.
Or right?
Currently, fabricated wooden boxes cast dark shadows on Warhol's catalogue of works in Sweden.
The majority of the ninety-four Brillo boxes listed there of the "type Stockholm" are forgeries.
After the Swedish newspaper "Expressen" a few months agove raised doubts about the authenticity of Andy Warhol's Brillo boxes in 1968, internal investigations at the Moderna Museet in Stockholm now come to a sobering conclusion: the six "Brillo-Boxes", which the former museum director Pontus Hultén bequeathed to the Moderna Museet in 1995 as a donation, are copies - and arguably the majority of the majority.
Prehistory is nested, a confusing concatenation of artistic and curatorial productions.
In 1964, Warhol created his first "Brillo-Boxes in New York," painted wooden boxes based on the red-blue-and-white design of the cardboard boxes of soap powder "Brillo."
An exhibition of his works at the Moderna Museet in 1968 exhibited - with Warhol's consent - hundreds of Brillo boxes made of cardboard: these did not come (for cost reasons) from Warhol, but were delivered directly from the Brillo cartonage factory.
These were not works of art, but about their models, mere exhibits.
At the same time, Warhol probably granted permission in 1968 to produce a few Brillo wooden boxes in Sweden, which, however, were not exhibited in the Moderna Museet.
Witnesses now speak of around fifteen boxes, which at that time rightly went down in art and auction history as authorized original Andy Warhol-Brillo boxes with the designation "Type Stockholm".
Some of them have raised more than €100 000 at auction.
The problem now is that the legendary museum director Pontus Hultén later falsely claimed that around a hundred wooden boxes were produced and exhibited in Sweden in 1968, which Warhol then handed over to him.
In fact, Hultén's 105 boxes weren't produced in Malmö until 1990 for an exhibition in St. Petersburg.
Warhol had already been dead for three years.
Lars Byström, chief conservator at the Moderna Museet, has examined not only the six museum-owned Brillo-Boxes but also three from Swedish private ownership, two of whom were made in Stockholm in 1968: their pressed chipboards were first primed and sanded, after which white oil paint was applied with a brush.
The structure of the 1990 copies, on the other hand, reveals that the paint was applied directly to the pressed chipboard with a painter's roll without priming - in addition, it is not acrylic paint.
"These boxes have not been authorized by the artist and should be removed from the official list of Andy Warhol's Brillo boxes," Moderna Museet director Lars Nittve states: "Unless the 'Andy Warhol Art Authentication Board' in New York comes to a different conclusion, Moderna Museet will classify the six boxes in its collection as copies/exhibition material."
Already in July, of course, the Authentication Board has announced to investigate the "serious accusations" that are raised in matters of soap powder boxes.
Since then, however, one shrouds oneself in relentless silence.
Rebelling Soldiers in Manila give up
In the Philippines, rebelling soldiers have given up entrenched themselves in a hotel in Manila and called for the overthrow of President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo.
He could not answer for bloodshed, leader Antonio Trillanes, who accused the president of corruption and electoral fraud, told reporters at the hotel.
Pro-government soldiers had previously stormed the hotel under tear gas deployment.
The approximately two dozen soldiers had been entrenched in the building for hours.
Around half of them were on trial on Thursday for a failed coup attempt in 2003 - among them Trillanes.
He used the trial to suddenly stand down with around 30 sympathizers.
The military policers, who were supposed to guard him, had apparently defected to his mutiny and joined his mutiny.
They invaded the Peninsula hotel and demanded the overthrow of President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo.
Loyal government forces cordoned off the district around the noble hotel.
The president convened an urgent meeting of the security cabinet.
Norberto Gonzales national security adviser condemned the soldiers' actions.
"People have had enough," Trillanes said.
“We call on people to join us.”
General Danilo Lim, who formerly commandeed the Scout Rangers' elite unit, said other army units wanted to join the march shortly.
Government critics had called for large demonstrations against the president for Friday.
Their government has been overshadowed by allegations of corruption for months.
Manila's financial district was already the main scene of the attempted mutiny of around 200 military personnel against Arroyo in 2003.
The uprising failed after just under 24 hours at the army's refusal to join.
Since 1986 there have been at least seven coup attempts against democratically elected governments in the Philippines.
United Internet becomes major shareholder at Versatel
United Internet has secured a more than 20 percent stake in telecoms company Versatel.
The average price per share is €14.46, the company announced on Wednesday evening.
20.05 percent of the share capital is now held.
There had previously been speculated about a takeover.
The option to increase the share of Versatel is still open.
The move aims to promote the consolidation of Germany’s DSL market, United Internet said.
Suspicions had already arisen on Wednesday about an entry, after Berenberg Bank increased its stake in the Düsseldorf-based network operator by a further 5 percent to 15.1 percent.
The money house considers the shares only a third party, it said in the financial market.
The previous day, United Internet had already invested almost 10 percent in the mobile phone company Drillisch.
As part of a capital increase, Drillisch has placed 17.4 million new shares worth 106.4 million euros.
United Internet bought 5.15 million shares for €31.4 million.
In addition, the companies have started discussions about marketing cooperations for DSL and mobile phone products.
"We are strengthening our partnership," a United Internet spokesman explained.
The two companies have established the joint venture MSP to take over the competitor Freenet.
For now, the two companies want to satisfy themselves with a stake of a good ten per cent of Freenet and make no use of their right to take over another 18.49 percent of Freenet, United Internet said on Thursday.
However, the two companies kept all options open, in particular to further increase the stake in Freenet AG, it said.
Conversations between United Internet and Freenet on a strategic partnership had ended in mid-November.
Freenet's poker, which has been going on for months and is only difficult to understand, is part of the consolidation of the German telecom market.
Freenet had 2.9 million mobile contract customers and 1.27 million DSL and full-connect customers at the end of the third quarter.
So far, United Internet and Drillisch's holding directly held 6.01 percent of Freenet's shares.
It has now been agreed that Drillisch will contribute another four percent.
The right to buy the 18.49 percent from investment firm Vatas had already been agreed in August.
